<article class="ltx_document ltx_authors_1line ltx_leqno">
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document">
Divergence-Kernel method for linear responses and diffusion models
</h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Angxiu Ni<sup id="id1" class="ltx_sup"><span id="id1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">1</span></sup>
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_address"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Address: </span><sup id="id2" class="ltx_sup">1</sup> Department of Mathematics, University of California, Irvine, USA
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_email"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Email address: </span><a href="mailto:angxiun@uci.edu">angxiun@uci.edu</a>
</span></span></span></span></div>
<div class="ltx_dates">Date: August 24, 2026</div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract.</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">We derive the divergence-kernel formula for the linear response (parameter-derivative of marginal or stationary distributions) of random dynamical systems, and formally pass to the continuous-time limit.
Our formula works for multiplicative and parameterized noise over any period of time; it does not require hyperbolicity.
Then we derive a pathwise Monte-Carlo algorithm for linear responses.
With this, we propose a forward-only diffusion generative model and test on simple problems.</p>
    
<p id="abstract1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="abstract1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">AMS subject classification numbers.</span>
60H07, 60J60, 65D25, 65C30, 65C05.</p>
    
<p id="abstract1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="abstract1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Keywords.</span>
Linear response,
Divergence method,
Likelihood ratio,
SDE,
Diffusion model.</p>
  
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1. </span>Introduction</h2>

<section id="S1.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">1.1. </span>Main results</h3>

<div id="S1.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This paper rigorously derives the divergence-kernel formula for the linear response of discrete-time random dynamical systems.
Then we formally pass to the continuous-time limit.
Our result gives a fundamental tool for the optimization of diffusion processes; in particular, from now on, we can use the gradient-based method to optimize marginal or stationary densities with respect to the diffusion coefficients.
In particular, we propose a simple framework for diffusion generative models.
Our main theoretical result is</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para"><span id="S1.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_ERROR undefined">{theorem}</span>
<p id="S1.SS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">[formal divergence-kernel formula for linear responses of SDEs]

Consider the SDE in <math id="S1.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>M</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{M}</annotation></semantics></math> parameterized by <math id="S1.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math>,</p>
<table id="S1.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="dx^{\gamma}_{t}=F^{\gamma}_{t}(x^{\gamma}_{t})dt+\sigma^{\gamma}_{t}(x^{\gamma}_{t})dB_{t}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>γ</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>F</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>γ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>γ</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>γ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>γ</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">dx^{\gamma}_{t}=F^{\gamma}_{t}(x^{\gamma}_{t})dt+\sigma^{\gamma}_{t}(x^{\gamma}_{t})dB_{t},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.SS1.p2.3" class="ltx_p">For any backward adapted process <math id="S1.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\alpha_{t}\}_{t=0}^{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\alpha_{t}\}_{t=0}^{T}</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="S1.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\nu_{t}\}_{t=0}^{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>ν</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\nu_{t}\}_{t=0}^{T}</annotation></semantics></math> be the forward covector process at <math id="S1.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma=0</annotation></semantics></math> such that</p>
<table id="S1.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{split}d\nu=\left((\nabla\sigma\nabla\sigma^{T}-\nabla F^{T}-\alpha)\nu-\nabla\textnormal{div}F+\nabla^{2}\sigma\nabla\sigma+\nabla\sigma\Delta\sigma\right)dt-(\nabla\sigma\nu^{T}+\nabla^{2}\sigma+\alpha\sigma^{-1})dB.\end{split}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable displaystyle="true"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mi>ν</mi><mo>=</mo><mo>(</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>σ</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>T</mi></msup><mo>−</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>F</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>T</mi></msup><mo>−</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mi>ν</mi><mo>−</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mtext>div</mtext><mi>F</mi><mo>+</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>σ</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo>+</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>σ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>σ</mi><mo>)</mo><mi>d</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>σ</mi><mi>ν</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>T</mi></msup><mo>+</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>σ</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>α</mi><mi>σ</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mi>d</mi><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{split}d\nu=\left((\nabla\sigma\nabla\sigma^{T}-\nabla F^{T}-\alpha)\nu-\nabla\textnormal{div}F+\nabla^{2}\sigma\nabla\sigma+\nabla\sigma\Delta\sigma\right)dt-(\nabla\sigma\nu^{T}+\nabla^{2}\sigma+\alpha\sigma^{-1})dB.\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.SS1.p2.4" class="ltx_p">with initial condition <math id="S1.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu_{0}=\nabla\log h_{0}(x_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>log</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu_{0}=\nabla\log h_{0}(x_{0})</annotation></semantics></math>,
where <math id="S1.SS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> is the probability density of <math id="S1.SS1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then
<math id="S1.SS1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla\log h_{t}(x_{t})=\mathbb{E}\left[\nu_{t}\middle|x_{t}\right]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>log</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><msub><mi>ν</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em" stretchy="true">|</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla\log h_{t}(x_{t})=\mathbb{E}\left[\nu_{t}\middle|x_{t}\right]</annotation></semantics></math>,
and</p>
<table id="S1.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{split}\delta\log h^{\gamma}_{T}(x_{T})=\mathbb{E}\left[\delta\log h^{\gamma}_{0}+\int_{t=0}^{T}\delta\sigma^{\gamma}\nabla\sigma^{T}\nu-\delta F^{\gamma T}\nu-\textnormal{div}\delta F^{\gamma}+\delta\sigma^{\gamma}\Delta\sigma+\nabla\sigma^{T}\nabla\delta\sigma^{\gamma}dt\right.\\
\left.-\int_{t=0}^{T}(\delta\sigma^{\gamma}\nu^{T}+\nabla\delta\sigma^{\gamma T})dB\middle|x_{T}\right].\end{split}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mi>log</mi><mi>h</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>γ</mi></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>[</mo><mi>δ</mi><mi>log</mi><mi>h</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>γ</mi></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>0</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.055em">+</mo><mo>∫</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>T</mi></msup><mi>δ</mi><mi>σ</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>γ</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>σ</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>T</mi></msup><mi>ν</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>δ</mi><mi>F</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msup><mi>ν</mi><mo>−</mo><mtext>div</mtext><mi>δ</mi><mi>F</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>γ</mi></msup><mo>+</mo><mi>δ</mi><mi>σ</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>γ</mi></msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>σ</mi><mo>+</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>σ</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>T</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>δ</mi><mi>σ</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>γ</mi></msup><mi>d</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">−</mo><mo rspace="0em">∫</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>T</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>δ</mi><mi>σ</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>γ</mi></msup><mi>ν</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>T</mi></msup><mo>+</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>δ</mi><mi>σ</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mi>d</mi><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em" stretchy="true">|</mo><mi>x</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>T</mi></msub><mo>]</mo><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{split}\delta\log h^{\gamma}_{T}(x_{T})=\mathbb{E}\left[\delta\log h^{\gamma}_{0}+\int_{t=0}^{T}\delta\sigma^{\gamma}\nabla\sigma^{T}\nu-\delta F^{\gamma T}\nu-\textnormal{div}\delta F^{\gamma}+\delta\sigma^{\gamma}\Delta\sigma+\nabla\sigma^{T}\nabla\delta\sigma^{\gamma}dt\right.\\
\left.-\int_{t=0}^{T}(\delta\sigma^{\gamma}\nu^{T}+\nabla\delta\sigma^{\gamma T})dB\middle|x_{T}\right].\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Here <math id="S1.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>σ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta\sigma</annotation></semantics></math> is the Laplacian of <math id="S1.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S1.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> is the Brownian motion, and the SDE is Ito.
The time integrations are the limits of <a href="#S3.E9" title="In 3.3. Preparations for SDEs ‣ 3. Derivation of the divergence-kernel formula for linear responses ‣ Divergence-Kernel method for linear responses and diffusion models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Equations</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a> and <a href="#S3.E10" title="Equation 10 ‣ Proof. ‣ 3.4. Continuous-time divergence-kernel formula for linear response ‣ 3. Derivation of the divergence-kernel formula for linear responses ‣ Divergence-Kernel method for linear responses and diffusion models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a>.
More generally, <math id="S1.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ν</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu</annotation></semantics></math> is an estimator for the score of the SDE at <math id="S1.SS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma=0</annotation></semantics></math>, which can be replaced by other divergence-kernel expressions from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The main advantages of this result are:</p>
<ol id="S1.I1" class="ltx_enumerate" style="--ltx-enum-leftmargin:0pt;">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1)</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">It computes derivative of the marginal density, not an averaged observable;</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(2)</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The expression in pathwise, so we can compute by Monte-Carlo type algorithms in high-dimensions;</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(3)</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The diffusion coefficient <math id="S1.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math> can depend on the parameter <math id="S1.I1.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math> and the location <math id="S1.I1.i3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>;</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(4)</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The size of the estimator does not grow exponentially to <math id="S1.I1.i4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>, so we can easily extend our result to stationary distributions (see <a href="#S3.SS4" title="3.4. Continuous-time divergence-kernel formula for linear response ‣ 3. Derivation of the divergence-kernel formula for linear responses ‣ Divergence-Kernel method for linear responses and diffusion models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.4</span></a>);</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i5" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(5)</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">It does not require hyperbolicity (in the sense of dynamical systems).</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">As a less obvious application, we use this result to construct a new diffusion generative model, where we directly optimize the marginal distribution to reduce its KL-divergence with respect to the data.
Importantly, we can optimize the diffusion coefficients of multiplicative noise.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S1.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">1.2. </span>Literature review</h3>

<div id="S1.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The marginal or stationary distribution of a random dynamical system is of central interest in applied sciences.
We are interested in the parameter-derivative <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta\log h_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta\log h_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> is the probability density at time <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>.
There are three basic methods for expressing and computing the linear response of random dynamical systems: the path-perturbation method (shortened as the path method), the divergence method, and the kernel-differentiation method (shortened as the kernel method).
The relation and difference among the three basic methods can be illustrated in a one-step system, which is explained in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The path-perturbation method computes the path-perturbation under fixed randomness (such as Brownian motion), then averages over many realizations of randomness.
This is also known as the ensemble method or the stochastic gradient method <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>, <a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite>.
It also includes the backpropagation method, which is the basic algorithm for machine learning.
However, it is expensive for chaotic or unstable system; the work-around is to artificially reduce the size of the path-perturbation, such as shadowing or clipping methods <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a>, <a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a>, <a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">30</a>, <a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite>, but they all introduce systematic errors.
Moreover, it computes the derivative of an averaged observable, but does not give the derivative of the probability density.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The divergence method is also known as the transfer operator method, since the perturbation of the measure transfer operator is some divergence <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>, <a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">36</a>, <a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">38</a>]</cite>.
However, it is expensive for high-dimensional systems with contracting directions.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The kernel-differentiation method works only for random systems.
It is also known as the Cameron-Martin-Girsanov theorem <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>, <a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite> or the likelihood ratio method <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">33</a>, <a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">31</a>, <a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite>.
However, it cannot handle multiplicative noise or perturbation on the diffusion coefficients.
It is also expensive when the noise is small.
We gave an ergodic version and a foliated version in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Mixing two basic methods can overcome some of their major shortcomings.
Our path-divergence method (also known as the fast response formula) gives the pointwise expression of linear responses of hyperbolic deterministic chaos <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>, <a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>, <a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>, <a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a>, <a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>, <a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>]</cite>.
It is good at high-dimensional and low-noise to deterministic systems.
It can be used to solve the optimal response problem in the hyperbolic setting <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite>.
However, it does not work when the hyperbolicity is poor <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>, <a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">37</a>]</cite>, and only the unstable part involves derivatives of densities.
The path-divergence idea was also used in the proof of the basic path-perturbation or divergence linear response formulas for hyperbolic systems <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>, <a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">34</a>, <a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>, <a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>, <a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS2.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS2.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Our path-kernel method in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>]</cite> gives the pathwise linear response formula of the diffusion coefficients.
It is good at systems with not too small noise and not too much unstableness, it does not require hyperbolicity.
However, it can be expensive when the noise is small and unstableness is big.
Moreover, only part of the expression is derivative on the terminal densities.
The path-kernel idea was also used in the Bismut-Elworthy-Li formula for the derivative with respect to the initial conditions <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>, <a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>, <a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">32</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS2.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS2.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Our divergence-kernel method in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>]</cite> gives the score (derivative of marginal density) of random dynamical systems and SDEs.
It works for non-hyperbolic and multiplicative noise systems.
It is good at systems with not too much contraction and not too little noise.
However, it can be expensive when the noise is small and the contraction is strong.
We use this result for the forward-backward diffusion models via nonlinear SDEs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite>.
This paper extends this result to linear responses.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS2.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS2.p8.1" class="ltx_p">Our new linear response formula opens up the possibilities of redesigning the current framework of diffusion models.
The current structure of the diffusion model involves a forward process and a backward process <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">35</a>, <a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite>.
Part of the reason is that previously we did not have a gradient tool to optimize marginal distributions with respect to the diffusion coefficient or under multiplicative noise.
If we can directly compute such a gradient, we can directly optimize the diffusion process so that the marginal distribution matches the data.
We will demonstrate this idea on some simple problems.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S1.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">1.3. </span>Structure of paper</h3>

<div id="S1.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><a href="#S2" title="2. Notations and assumptions ‣ Divergence-Kernel method for linear responses and diffusion models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> defines some basic notation and states some basic assumptions on regularities.
<a href="#S3.SS1" title="3.1. Divergence formula One-step system ‣ 3. Derivation of the divergence-kernel formula for linear responses ‣ Divergence-Kernel method for linear responses and diffusion models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a> derives our the divergence formula for the linear response of one-step random systems.
<a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2. Discrete-time divergence-kernel formula for linear responses ‣ 3. Derivation of the divergence-kernel formula for linear responses ‣ Divergence-Kernel method for linear responses and diffusion models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a> derives our results for discrete-time random dynamical systems by combining the one-step divergence linear response formula with the multi-step divergence-kernel formula for scores.
<a href="#S3.SS3" title="3.3. Preparations for SDEs ‣ 3. Derivation of the divergence-kernel formula for linear responses ‣ Divergence-Kernel method for linear responses and diffusion models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a> recalls some useful facts from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>]</cite> about scores for SDEs.
<a href="#S3.SS4" title="3.4. Continuous-time divergence-kernel formula for linear response ‣ 3. Derivation of the divergence-kernel formula for linear responses ‣ Divergence-Kernel method for linear responses and diffusion models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.4</span></a> formally passes the linear response formula to the limit of SDEs.
<a href="#S3.SS5" title="3.5. Discussions ‣ 3. Derivation of the divergence-kernel formula for linear responses ‣ Divergence-Kernel method for linear responses and diffusion models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.5</span></a> discusses about how to use this result.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><a href="#S4" title="4. Numerical examples ‣ Divergence-Kernel method for linear responses and diffusion models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> uses the new formulas to numerically compute the linear responses of two model systems.
<a href="#S4.SS1" title="4.1. 1-dimensional process with multiplicative noise ‣ 4. Numerical examples ‣ Divergence-Kernel method for linear responses and diffusion models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a> considers a 1-dimensional process with multiplicative noise.
For this 1-d example, we can plot the parameter-derivative of the density.
<a href="#S4.SS2" title="4.2. 40-dimensional Lorenz 96 system with multiplicative noise ‣ 4. Numerical examples ‣ Divergence-Kernel method for linear responses and diffusion models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a> computes the linear response of the stationary measure of the 40-dimensional Lorenz 96 model with multiplicative noise.
These examples can not be solved by previous methods since the system is high-dimensional, has multiplicative noise, has contracting directions, and is non-hyperbolic.
Moreover, compared with our path-kernel methods, here we can compute the derivative of the density.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><a href="#S5" title="5. A simple forward-only diffusion model ‣ Divergence-Kernel method for linear responses and diffusion models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> proposes a diffusion model which involves only two forward processes.
It uses the divergence-kernel formula (<span id="S1.SS3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">not</span> the backpropagation method, which belongs to the path-perturbation method) to perform a gradient descent on the parameters of an SDE.
Its loss function is the KL-divergence between the data and the marginal distribution of the SDE.
The logic is more straightforward than the current diffusion models, and the memory cost is less.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><a href="#A1" title="Appendix A Kernel-differentiation method ‣ Divergence-Kernel method for linear responses and diffusion models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a> briefly goes through the kernel-differentiation method and explains its problem when applied to linear responses of high-dimensional systems with respect to scalar diffusion coefficients.
We also derive the conventional likelihood ratio formula for additive noise systems using our notation.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2. </span>Notations and assumptions</h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We define some geometric notations for spatial derivatives.
In this paper, the ambient space is the <math id="S2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional Euclidean space <math id="S2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>M</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{M}</annotation></semantics></math>.
We denote both vectors and covectors by column vectors in <math id="S2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>M</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{M}</annotation></semantics></math>.
The product between a covector <math id="S2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ν</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu</annotation></semantics></math> and a vector <math id="S2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> is denoted by <math id="S2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\cdot" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>⋅</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\cdot</annotation></semantics></math>,
that is,</p>
<table id="S2.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{split}\nu\cdot v:=v\cdot\nu:=\nu^{T}v:=v^{T}\nu.\end{split}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable displaystyle="true"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>ν</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>ν</mi></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ν</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>v</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ν</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{split}\nu\cdot v:=v\cdot\nu:=\nu^{T}v:=v^{T}\nu.\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Here <math id="S2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v^{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>v</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v^{T}</annotation></semantics></math> is the transpose of matrices or (co)vectors.
Then we use <math id="S2.p1.m8" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\left\langle,\right\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>⟨</mo><mo>,</mo><mo>⟩</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left\langle,\right\rangle</annotation></semantics></math> to denote the inner-product between two vectors</p>
<table id="S2.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{split}\left\langle u,v\right\rangle:=u^{T}v.\end{split}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable displaystyle="true"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>⟨</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo>⟩</mo></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>u</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{split}\left\langle u,v\right\rangle:=u^{T}v.\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p1.3" class="ltx_p">Note that <math id="S2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\Delta B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\Delta B}</annotation></semantics></math> may be either a vector or a covector.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The score function is <math id="S2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla\log{h_{T}}=\frac{\nabla h_{T}}{h_{T}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>log</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla\log{h_{T}}=\frac{\nabla h_{T}}{h_{T}}</annotation></semantics></math>, where</p>
<table id="S2.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_eqnarray ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.Ex6"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\nabla(\cdot):=\frac{\partial(\cdot)}{\partial x},\quad\nabla_{v}(\cdot):=\nabla(\cdot)v:=\frac{\partial(\cdot)}{\partial x}v," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>∇</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><msub><mo>∇</mo><mi>v</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mo>∇</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>:=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\nabla(\cdot):=\frac{\partial(\cdot)}{\partial x},\quad\nabla_{v}(\cdot):=\nabla(\cdot)v:=\frac{\partial(\cdot)}{\partial x}v,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">Here <math id="S2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla_{Y}X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><mi>Y</mi></msub><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla_{Y}X</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the (Riemann) derivative of the tensor field <math id="S2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> along the direction of <math id="S2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math>.
It is convenient to think that <math id="S2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∇</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla</annotation></semantics></math> always adds a covariant component to the tensor.
The divergence of a vector field <math id="S2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is defined by</p>
<table id="S2.Ex7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{split}\textnormal{div}X:=\sum_{i=1}^{M}\nabla_{e_{i}}X,\end{split}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable displaystyle="true"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>div</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">:=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>M</mi></munderover><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><mi>X</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{split}\textnormal{div}X:=\sum_{i=1}^{M}\nabla_{e_{i}}X,\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p2.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is the <math id="S2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>-th unit vector of the canonical basis of <math id="S2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>M</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{M}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">For a map <math id="S2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="S2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{*}</annotation></semantics></math> be the Jacobian matrix, or the pushforward operator on vectors; readers may also denote this by <math id="S2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla g</annotation></semantics></math>.
We define the covector <math id="S2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textnormal{div}g_{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>div</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textnormal{div}g_{*}</annotation></semantics></math>, the divergence of the Jacobian matrix <math id="S2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{*}</annotation></semantics></math>, as</p>
<table id="S2.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(1)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{split}\textnormal{div}g_{*}:=\nabla\log\left|g_{*}\right|:=\frac{\nabla\left|g_{*}\right|}{\left|g_{*}\right|},\end{split}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable displaystyle="true"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>div</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>log</mi></mrow><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>|</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo>|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo>∇</mo><mrow><mo>|</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo>|</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>|</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo>|</mo></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{split}\textnormal{div}g_{*}:=\nabla\log\left|g_{*}\right|:=\frac{\nabla\left|g_{*}\right|}{\left|g_{*}\right|},\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p3.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left|g_{*}\right|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>|</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo>|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left|g_{*}\right|</annotation></semantics></math> is the determinant of the matrix.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">For discrete-time results in <a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2. Discrete-time divergence-kernel formula for linear responses ‣ 3. Derivation of the divergence-kernel formula for linear responses ‣ Divergence-Kernel method for linear responses and diffusion models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>, we assume that</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmassumptionx1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_assumption">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmassumptionx1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Assumption</span></span><span id="Thmassumptionx1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmassumptionx1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmassumptionx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmassumptionx1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The density of the initial distribution <math id="Thmassumptionx1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>h</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, the probability kernel <math id="Thmassumptionx1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>, drift <math id="Thmassumptionx1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math>, and diffusion <math id="Thmassumptionx1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math> are <math id="Thmassumptionx1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> jointly in <math id="Thmassumptionx1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmassumptionx1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Our derivation is rigorous for the discrete-time case, but is <span id="S2.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">formal</span> when passing to the continuous-time limit, so we do not list the technical assumptions here, and leave the rigorous proof to a later paper.
We just assume that all integrations, averages, and change of limits are legit.
We still organize our continuous-time results by lemmas and theorems, but this is just a style to organize the paper and does not imply rigorousness.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3. </span>Derivation of the divergence-kernel formula for linear responses</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">There are two basic ingredients to our work.
The first is the divergence formula for <math id="S3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta\log h^{\gamma}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msubsup><mi>h</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>γ</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta\log h^{\gamma}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> in the one-step system, which we shall derive now.
The second is the divergence-kernel formula for the scores in many-step systems and its continuous-time limit; this result is from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1. </span>Divergence formula One-step system</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x^{\gamma}_{0}\sim h^{\gamma}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>γ</mi></msubsup><mo>∼</mo><msubsup><mi>h</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>γ</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x^{\gamma}_{0}\sim h^{\gamma}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b^{\gamma}_{0}\sim k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>b</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>γ</mi></msubsup><mo>∼</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b^{\gamma}_{0}\sim k</annotation></semantics></math>, the one-step dynamics is</p>
<table id="S3.EGx2" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_eqnarray ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.Ex8"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle x^{\gamma}_{1}=f^{\gamma}_{0}(x^{\gamma}_{0})+\sigma^{\gamma}_{0}(x^{\gamma}_{0})b_{0}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>γ</mi></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>γ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>γ</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>γ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>γ</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle x^{\gamma}_{1}=f^{\gamma}_{0}(x^{\gamma}_{0})+\sigma^{\gamma}_{0}(x^{\gamma}_{0})b_{0}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Here <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math> is the parameter; when we omit <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math> in our notation, it takes the default value <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>.
We sometimes omit the subscripts that indicate the time step of <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math>, which are the same as the time step of the variable <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>.
Let <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h^{\gamma}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>h</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>γ</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{\gamma}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the density of <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x^{\gamma}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>γ</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x^{\gamma}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, which has a natural expression:</p>
<table id="S3.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(2)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h^{\gamma}_{1}(x_{1})=\int h^{\gamma}_{0}(x_{0})p^{\gamma}_{0}(x_{0},x_{1})dx_{0}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>h</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>γ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>h</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>γ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>γ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{\gamma}_{1}(x_{1})=\int h^{\gamma}_{0}(x_{0})p^{\gamma}_{0}(x_{0},x_{1})dx_{0}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.3" class="ltx_p">where the probability kernel is</p>
<table id="S3.Ex9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{split}p^{\gamma}_{0}(x_{0},x_{1}):=(\sigma^{\gamma}_{0})^{-M}(x_{0})k\left(\frac{x_{1}-f^{\gamma}_{0}(x_{0})}{\sigma^{\gamma}_{0}(x_{0})}\right).\end{split}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable displaystyle="true"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>γ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>γ</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>γ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>γ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{split}p^{\gamma}_{0}(x_{0},x_{1}):=(\sigma^{\gamma}_{0})^{-M}(x_{0})k\left(\frac{x_{1}-f^{\gamma}_{0}(x_{0})}{\sigma^{\gamma}_{0}(x_{0})}\right).\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Change the variable <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{0}=(x_{1}-f^{\gamma}_{0}(x_{0}))/\sigma^{\gamma}_{0}(x_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>γ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>γ</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{0}=(x_{1}-f^{\gamma}_{0}(x_{0}))/\sigma^{\gamma}_{0}(x_{0})</annotation></semantics></math> under fixed <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Define</p>
<table id="S3.Ex10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{split}x_{1}=g^{\gamma}_{b_{0}}(x_{0}):=f^{\gamma}_{0}(x_{0})+\sigma^{\gamma}_{0}(x_{0})b_{0}\end{split}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable displaystyle="true"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>g</mi><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mi>γ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>γ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>γ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{split}x_{1}=g^{\gamma}_{b_{0}}(x_{0}):=f^{\gamma}_{0}(x_{0})+\sigma^{\gamma}_{0}(x_{0})b_{0}\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">Since <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> is fixed, <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> are related by</p>
<table id="S3.Ex11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{split}0=dx_{1}=g^{\gamma}_{b_{0}*}(x_{0})dx_{0}+\sigma^{\gamma}_{0}(x_{0})db_{0},\end{split}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable displaystyle="true"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>=</mo><mi>d</mi><mi>x</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>g</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>γ</mi></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>b</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em">∗</mo></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mi>d</mi><mi>x</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>σ</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>γ</mi></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mi>d</mi><mi>b</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{split}0=dx_{1}=g^{\gamma}_{b_{0}*}(x_{0})dx_{0}+\sigma^{\gamma}_{0}(x_{0})db_{0},\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="g^{\gamma}_{b_{0}*}:=\nabla f^{\gamma}+b_{0}(\nabla\sigma^{\gamma})^{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>g</mi><mrow><mi>b</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em">∗</mo></mrow><mi>γ</mi></msubsup><mo>:=</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msup><mi>f</mi><mi>γ</mi></msup><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msup><mi>σ</mi><mi>γ</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>T</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g^{\gamma}_{b_{0}*}:=\nabla f^{\gamma}+b_{0}(\nabla\sigma^{\gamma})^{T}</annotation></semantics></math> is the pushforward operator of <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{b_{0}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{b_{0}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Changing the variable causes a nontrivial Jacobian determinant.</p>
<table id="S3.Ex12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{split}\left|\frac{db_{0}}{dx_{0}}\right|=(\sigma_{0}^{\gamma})^{-M}(x_{0})\left|g^{\gamma}_{b_{0}*}(x_{0})\right|,\end{split}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable displaystyle="true"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em">|</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo fence="false">|</mo><mo lspace="0.167em">=</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>0</mn></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>γ</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em">|</mo><mi>g</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>γ</mi></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>b</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em">∗</mo></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em">|</mo><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{split}\left|\frac{db_{0}}{dx_{0}}\right|=(\sigma_{0}^{\gamma})^{-M}(x_{0})\left|g^{\gamma}_{b_{0}*}(x_{0})\right|,\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.4" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m9" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\left|g^{\gamma}_{b_{0}*}\right|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em">|</mo><mi>g</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>γ</mi></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>b</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em">∗</mo></mrow></msub><mo fence="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left|g^{\gamma}_{b_{0}*}\right|</annotation></semantics></math> is the determinant.
So we can express <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h^{\gamma}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>h</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>γ</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{\gamma}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> by an integration over <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>,</p>
<table id="S3.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(3)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{split}h^{\gamma}_{1}(x_{1})=\int h^{\gamma}_{0}(x^{\gamma}_{0})k(b_{0})\left|g^{\gamma}_{b_{0}*}(x^{\gamma}_{0})\right|^{-1}db_{0},\quad\textnormal{where}\quad x^{\gamma}_{0}=(g^{\gamma}_{b_{0}})^{-1}(x_{1}).\end{split}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable displaystyle="true"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mi>h</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>γ</mi></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mo>∫</mo><mi>h</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>γ</mi></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>γ</mi></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>b</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em">|</mo><mi>g</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>γ</mi></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>b</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em">∗</mo></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>γ</mi></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em">|</mo><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mi>d</mi><mi>b</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>0</mn></msub><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mtext>where</mtext><mi>x</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>γ</mi></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>γ</mi></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{split}h^{\gamma}_{1}(x_{1})=\int h^{\gamma}_{0}(x^{\gamma}_{0})k(b_{0})\left|g^{\gamma}_{b_{0}*}(x^{\gamma}_{0})\right|^{-1}db_{0},\quad\textnormal{where}\quad x^{\gamma}_{0}=(g^{\gamma}_{b_{0}})^{-1}(x_{1}).\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.5" class="ltx_p">For now, we assume <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g^{\gamma}_{b_{0}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>g</mi><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mi>γ</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g^{\gamma}_{b_{0}}</annotation></semantics></math> is injective, since this is the case when we discretize SDEs; if it is <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>-to-1, then we need to further sum over its preimage.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para"><span id="S3.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_ERROR undefined">{lemma}</span>
<p id="S3.SS1.p3.2" class="ltx_p">[Divergence formula for one-step linear response]</p>
<table id="S3.Ex13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{split}\delta\log h^{\gamma}_{1}(x_{1})=\mathbb{E}\left[\frac{\delta h^{\gamma}_{0}+\nabla_{\delta x^{\gamma}_{0}}h_{0}}{h_{0}}(x_{0})-\frac{\delta\left|g^{\gamma}_{b_{0}*}\right|+\nabla_{\delta x^{\gamma}_{0}}\left|g_{b_{0}*}\right|}{\left|g_{b_{0}*}\right|}(x_{0})\middle|x_{1}\right],\end{split}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable displaystyle="true"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msubsup><mi>h</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>γ</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>h</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>γ</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>γ</mi></msubsup></mrow></msub><msub><mi>h</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em">|</mo><mi>g</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>γ</mi></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>b</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em">∗</mo></mrow></msub><mo fence="false">|</mo><mo lspace="0em">+</mo><mo>∇</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>γ</mi></msubsup></mrow></msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em">|</mo><mi>g</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>b</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em">∗</mo></mrow></msub><mo fence="false">|</mo></mrow><mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em">|</mo><mi>g</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>b</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em">∗</mo></mrow></msub><mo fence="false">|</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em" stretchy="true">|</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{split}\delta\log h^{\gamma}_{1}(x_{1})=\mathbb{E}\left[\frac{\delta h^{\gamma}_{0}+\nabla_{\delta x^{\gamma}_{0}}h_{0}}{h_{0}}(x_{0})-\frac{\delta\left|g^{\gamma}_{b_{0}*}\right|+\nabla_{\delta x^{\gamma}_{0}}\left|g_{b_{0}*}\right|}{\left|g_{b_{0}*}\right|}(x_{0})\middle|x_{1}\right],\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p3.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\delta x^{\gamma}_{0}=-g^{-1}_{b_{0}*}\delta g^{\gamma}_{b_{0}}(x_{0})." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>γ</mi></msubsup><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><msubsup><mi>g</mi><mrow><mi>b</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em">∗</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mi>δ</mi><msubsup><mi>g</mi><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mi>γ</mi></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta x^{\gamma}_{0}=-g^{-1}_{b_{0}*}\delta g^{\gamma}_{b_{0}}(x_{0}).</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S3.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">Differentiate <a href="#S3.E3" title="In 3.1. Divergence formula One-step system ‣ 3. Derivation of the divergence-kernel formula for linear responses ‣ Divergence-Kernel method for linear responses and diffusion models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Equation</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> by <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math>, note that the dummy variable <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> is not differentiated; the derivative hits <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h^{\gamma}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>h</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>γ</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{\gamma}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="g^{\gamma}_{b_{0}*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>γ</mi></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>b</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em">∗</mo></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g^{\gamma}_{b_{0}*}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x^{\gamma}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>γ</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x^{\gamma}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>.
We get</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex14" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{split}\delta h^{\gamma}_{1}(x_{1})=\int\left(\frac{\delta h^{\gamma}_{0}+\nabla_{\delta x^{\gamma}_{0}}h_{0}}{h_{0}}(x_{0})-\frac{\delta\left|g^{\gamma}_{b_{0}*}\right|+\nabla_{\delta x^{\gamma}_{0}}\left|g_{b_{0}*}\right|}{\left|g_{b_{0}*}\right|}(x_{0})\right)h_{0}(x_{0})k(b_{0})\left|g_{b_{0}*}(x_{0})\right|^{-1}db_{0},\end{split}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable displaystyle="true"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mi>h</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>γ</mi></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mo rspace="0em">∫</mo><mo>(</mo><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>h</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>γ</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>γ</mi></msubsup></mrow></msub><msub><mi>h</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mfrac><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em">|</mo><mi>g</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>γ</mi></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>b</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em">∗</mo></mrow></msub><mo fence="false">|</mo><mo lspace="0em">+</mo><mo>∇</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>γ</mi></msubsup></mrow></msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em">|</mo><mi>g</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>b</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em">∗</mo></mrow></msub><mo fence="false">|</mo></mrow><mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em">|</mo><mi>g</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>b</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em">∗</mo></mrow></msub><mo fence="false">|</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>)</mo><mi>h</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>b</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em">|</mo><mi>g</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>b</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em">∗</mo></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em">|</mo><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mi>d</mi><mi>b</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{split}\delta h^{\gamma}_{1}(x_{1})=\int\left(\frac{\delta h^{\gamma}_{0}+\nabla_{\delta x^{\gamma}_{0}}h_{0}}{h_{0}}(x_{0})-\frac{\delta\left|g^{\gamma}_{b_{0}*}\right|+\nabla_{\delta x^{\gamma}_{0}}\left|g_{b_{0}*}\right|}{\left|g_{b_{0}*}\right|}(x_{0})\right)h_{0}(x_{0})k(b_{0})\left|g_{b_{0}*}(x_{0})\right|^{-1}db_{0},\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p4.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p4.2.1" class="ltx_text">To get <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta x^{\gamma}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>γ</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta x^{\gamma}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, differentiate <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{1}=g^{\gamma}_{b_{0}}(x^{\gamma}_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>g</mi><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mi>γ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>γ</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{1}=g^{\gamma}_{b_{0}}(x^{\gamma}_{0})</annotation></semantics></math>, note that <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> is fixed, we get</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex15" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{split}0=\delta g^{\gamma}_{b_{0}}(x_{0})+g_{b_{0}*}\delta x^{\gamma}_{0}.\end{split}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable displaystyle="true"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>=</mo><mi>δ</mi><mi>g</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>γ</mi></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>+</mo><mi>g</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>b</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em">∗</mo></mrow></msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>x</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>γ</mi></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{split}0=\delta g^{\gamma}_{b_{0}}(x_{0})+g_{b_{0}*}\delta x^{\gamma}_{0}.\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p4.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p4.3.1" class="ltx_text">Hence, <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m9" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\delta x^{\gamma}_{0}=-g^{-1}_{b_{0}*}\delta g^{\gamma}_{b_{0}}(x_{0})." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>γ</mi></msubsup><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><msubsup><mi>g</mi><mrow><mi>b</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em">∗</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mi>δ</mi><msubsup><mi>g</mi><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mi>γ</mi></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta x^{\gamma}_{0}=-g^{-1}_{b_{0}*}\delta g^{\gamma}_{b_{0}}(x_{0}).</annotation></semantics></math>
Then substitute to prove the lemma.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">We do not work on the divergence-kernel method for linear responses in one-step, since it is not directly used.
The reason is that the kernel formula for linear responses (see <a href="#A1.SS1" title="A.1. One-step results ‣ Appendix A Kernel-differentiation method ‣ Divergence-Kernel method for linear responses and diffusion models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.1</span></a> in <a href="#A1" title="Appendix A Kernel-differentiation method ‣ Divergence-Kernel method for linear responses and diffusion models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a>) has a term with factor <math id="S3.SS1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>, which can be expensive (since large terms require many samples to compute averages) for important applications.
The kernel formula for scores does not have this large term.
Hence, we choose to use the divergence formula for the linear response and the divergence-kernel formula for the scores.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">That being said, we can certainly mix the kernel and divergence linear response formulas in one-step, then repeatedly apply to many-step systems, and then pass to the continuous-time limit.
That approach might be beneficial if we later work with anisotropic noise, where <math id="S3.SS1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math> is a matrix, and each parameter controls only a low-dimensional structure, so we do not have the <math id="S3.SS1.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> factor.
Another scenario where this approach might be more useful is that the noise is low-dimensional.
Bot situations incur other difficulties, so we will defer to later papers.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2. </span>Discrete-time divergence-kernel formula for linear responses</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Starting from the initial density <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x^{\gamma}_{0}\sim h^{\gamma}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>γ</mi></msubsup><mo>∼</mo><msubsup><mi>h</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>γ</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x^{\gamma}_{0}\sim h^{\gamma}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, consider the discrete-time random dynamical system</p>
<table id="S3.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(4)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{split}x^{\gamma}_{n+1}=f^{\gamma}_{n}(x^{\gamma}_{n})+\sigma^{\gamma}_{n}(x^{\gamma}_{n})b_{n},\end{split}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable displaystyle="true"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>γ</mi></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mi>n</mi><mi>γ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi><mi>γ</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>n</mi><mi>γ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi><mi>γ</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{split}x^{\gamma}_{n+1}=f^{\gamma}_{n}(x^{\gamma}_{n})+\sigma^{\gamma}_{n}(x^{\gamma}_{n})b_{n},\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> is i.i.d. distributed according to the kernel density <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>.
Denote the density of <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x^{\gamma}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi><mi>γ</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x^{\gamma}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> by <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h^{\gamma}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>h</mi><mi>n</mi><mi>γ</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{\gamma}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>.
The system runs for a total of <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> steps.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We use the divergence formula in one-step, then substitute the recent divergence-kernel formula for the score at <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma=0</annotation></semantics></math>.
Note that the score formula contains the kernel-differentiation formula for the score, but not the kernel-differentiation formula for linear responses.
Hence, we do not have the term involving <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> which can potentially get very large for scalar diffusion coefficients.
First, recall the following:</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmdefinition1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmdefinition1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 1</span></span><span id="Thmdefinition1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmdefinition1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmdefinition1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmdefinition1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">We say that a process <math id="Thmdefinition1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\alpha_{n}\}_{n=0}^{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\alpha_{n}\}_{n=0}^{N}</annotation></semantics></math> is backward adapted, or adapted to the backward filtration, if <math id="Thmdefinition1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> belongs to the <math id="Thmdefinition1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math>-algebra <math id="Thmdefinition1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma(x_{n},\ldots,x_{N})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma(x_{n},\ldots,x_{N})</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para"><span id="S3.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_ERROR undefined">{lemma}</span>
<p id="S3.SS2.p3.2" class="ltx_p">[N-step forward divergence-kernel formula for score <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>]</cite>] 
For any backward adapted process <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\alpha_{n}\}_{n=0}^{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\alpha_{n}\}_{n=0}^{N}</annotation></semantics></math>,
let <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\nu_{n}\}_{n=0}^{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>ν</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\nu_{n}\}_{n=0}^{N}</annotation></semantics></math> be the forward covector process</p>
<table id="S3.Ex16" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{split}\nu_{0}=\nabla\log h_{0}(x_{0}),\quad\textnormal{}\quad\nu_{n+1}=(1-\alpha_{n+1})g_{b_{n}}^{*-1}\left(\nu_{n}-\textnormal{div}g_{b_{n}*}(x_{n})\right)+\frac{\alpha_{n+1}\nabla\log k(b_{n})}{\sigma_{n}(x_{n})}.\end{split}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable displaystyle="true"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mi>ν</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>log</mi><mi>h</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow></mrow><mi>ν</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>α</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mi>g</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∗</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msup><mo>(</mo><mi>ν</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mtext>div</mtext><mi>g</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>b</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em">∗</mo></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>)</mo><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>α</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>log</mi><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>b</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{split}\nu_{0}=\nabla\log h_{0}(x_{0}),\quad\textnormal{}\quad\nu_{n+1}=(1-\alpha_{n+1})g_{b_{n}}^{*-1}\left(\nu_{n}-\textnormal{div}g_{b_{n}*}(x_{n})\right)+\frac{\alpha_{n+1}\nabla\log k(b_{n})}{\sigma_{n}(x_{n})}.\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p3.3" class="ltx_p">Then for any <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>,</p>
<table id="S3.Ex17" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{split}\nabla\log h_{N}(x_{N})=\mathbb{E}\left[\nu_{N}\middle|x_{N}\right].\end{split}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable displaystyle="true"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>log</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>N</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><msub><mi>ν</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em" stretchy="true">|</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{split}\nabla\log h_{N}(x_{N})=\mathbb{E}\left[\nu_{N}\middle|x_{N}\right].\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para"><span id="S3.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_ERROR undefined">{theorem}</span>
<p id="S3.SS2.p4.2" class="ltx_p">[N-step divergence-kernel formula for linear responses]</p>
<table id="S3.Ex18" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{split}\delta\log h^{\gamma}_{N}(x_{N})=\mathbb{E}\left[\delta\log h^{\gamma}_{0}+\sum_{n=0}^{N-1}\left(\nu_{n}-\nabla\log|g_{b_{n}*}|\right)\cdot\delta x^{\gamma}_{n}-\delta\log|g^{\gamma}_{b_{n}*}|\middle|x_{N}\right].\end{split}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable displaystyle="true"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mi>log</mi><mi>h</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>γ</mi></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>N</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>N</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>[</mo><mi>δ</mi><mi>log</mi><mi>h</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>γ</mi></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>0</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.055em">+</mo><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>(</mo><mi>ν</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>log</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>g</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>b</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em">∗</mo></mrow></msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo rspace="0.055em">)</mo><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>δ</mi><mi>x</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>γ</mi></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>δ</mi><mi>log</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>g</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>γ</mi></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>b</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em">∗</mo></mrow></msub><mo fence="false" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em" stretchy="true">|</mo><mi>x</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>N</mi></msub><mo>]</mo><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{split}\delta\log h^{\gamma}_{N}(x_{N})=\mathbb{E}\left[\delta\log h^{\gamma}_{0}+\sum_{n=0}^{N-1}\left(\nu_{n}-\nabla\log|g_{b_{n}*}|\right)\cdot\delta x^{\gamma}_{n}-\delta\log|g^{\gamma}_{b_{n}*}|\middle|x_{N}\right].\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p4.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\delta x^{\gamma}_{n}=-g^{-1}_{b_{n}*}\delta g^{\gamma}_{b_{n}}(x_{n})." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi><mi>γ</mi></msubsup><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><msubsup><mi>g</mi><mrow><mi>b</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em">∗</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mi>δ</mi><msubsup><mi>g</mi><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>γ</mi></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta x^{\gamma}_{n}=-g^{-1}_{b_{n}*}\delta g^{\gamma}_{b_{n}}(x_{n}).</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S3.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text">By <a href="#S3.SS1" title="3.1. Divergence formula One-step system ‣ 3. Derivation of the divergence-kernel formula for linear responses ‣ Divergence-Kernel method for linear responses and diffusion models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>, denote <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\delta x^{\gamma}_{N-1}:=-g^{-1}_{b_{N-1}*}\delta g^{\gamma}_{b_{N-1}}(x_{N-1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>γ</mi></msubsup><mo rspace="0em">:=</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><msubsup><mi>g</mi><mrow><mi>b</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em">∗</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mi>δ</mi><msubsup><mi>g</mi><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mi>γ</mi></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta x^{\gamma}_{N-1}:=-g^{-1}_{b_{N-1}*}\delta g^{\gamma}_{b_{N-1}}(x_{N-1})</annotation></semantics></math>,</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex19" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{split}\delta\log h^{\gamma}_{N}(x_{N})=\mathbb{E}\left[\delta\log h^{\gamma}_{N-1}+\nabla_{\delta x^{\gamma}_{N-1}}\log h_{N-1}-\delta\log|g^{\gamma}_{b_{N-1}*}|-\nabla_{\delta x^{\gamma}_{N-1}}\log|g_{b_{N-1}*}|\middle|x_{N}\right],\end{split}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable displaystyle="true"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mi>log</mi><mi>h</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>γ</mi></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>N</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>N</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>[</mo><mi>δ</mi><mi>log</mi><mi>h</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>γ</mi></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>γ</mi></msubsup></mrow></msub><mi>log</mi><mi>h</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>δ</mi><mi>log</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>g</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>γ</mi></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>b</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em">∗</mo></mrow></msub><mo fence="false" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>γ</mi></msubsup></mrow></msub><mi>log</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>g</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>b</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em">∗</mo></mrow></msub><mo fence="false" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em" stretchy="true">|</mo><mi>x</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>N</mi></msub><mo>]</mo><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{split}\delta\log h^{\gamma}_{N}(x_{N})=\mathbb{E}\left[\delta\log h^{\gamma}_{N-1}+\nabla_{\delta x^{\gamma}_{N-1}}\log h_{N-1}-\delta\log|g^{\gamma}_{b_{N-1}*}|-\nabla_{\delta x^{\gamma}_{N-1}}\log|g_{b_{N-1}*}|\middle|x_{N}\right],\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p5.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p5.2.1" class="ltx_text">where all quantities are evaluated at <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{N-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{N-1}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text">To handle the term involving <math id="S3.SS2.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla\log h_{N-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>log</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla\log h_{N-1}</annotation></semantics></math>, note that it does not depend on <math id="S3.SS2.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{N}</annotation></semantics></math>, so</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex20" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{split}\mathbb{E}\left[\nabla_{\delta x^{\gamma}_{N-1}}\log h_{N-1}(x_{N-1})\middle|x_{N}\right]=\mathbb{E}\left[\mathbb{E}\left[\nabla\log h_{N-1}(x_{N-1})\middle|x_{N-1},x_{N}\right]\cdot\delta x^{\gamma}_{N-1}\middle|x_{N}\right].\end{split}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable displaystyle="true"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>[</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>γ</mi></msubsup></mrow></msub><mi>log</mi><mi>h</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em" stretchy="true">|</mo><mi>x</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>N</mi></msub><mo>]</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>[</mo><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>[</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>log</mi><mi>h</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em" stretchy="true">|</mo><mi>x</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>N</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.055em">]</mo><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>δ</mi><mi>x</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>γ</mi></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em" stretchy="true">|</mo><mi>x</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>N</mi></msub><mo>]</mo><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{split}\mathbb{E}\left[\nabla_{\delta x^{\gamma}_{N-1}}\log h_{N-1}(x_{N-1})\middle|x_{N}\right]=\mathbb{E}\left[\mathbb{E}\left[\nabla\log h_{N-1}(x_{N-1})\middle|x_{N-1},x_{N}\right]\cdot\delta x^{\gamma}_{N-1}\middle|x_{N}\right].\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p6.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p6.2.1" class="ltx_text">use <a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2. Discrete-time divergence-kernel formula for linear responses ‣ 3. Derivation of the divergence-kernel formula for linear responses ‣ Divergence-Kernel method for linear responses and diffusion models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a> (note that it requires conditioning on all steps <math id="S3.SS2.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\geq N-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\geq N-1</annotation></semantics></math>),</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex21" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex21.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{split}\mathbb{E}\left[\nabla\log h_{N-1}(x_{N-1})\middle|x_{N-1},x_{N}\right]=\mathbb{E}\left[\nu_{N-1}\middle|x_{N-1},x_{N}\right].\end{split}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable displaystyle="true"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>[</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>log</mi><mi>h</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em" stretchy="true">|</mo><mi>x</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>N</mi></msub><mo>]</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>[</mo><mi>ν</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em" stretchy="true">|</mo><mi>x</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>N</mi></msub><mo>]</mo><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{split}\mathbb{E}\left[\nabla\log h_{N-1}(x_{N-1})\middle|x_{N-1},x_{N}\right]=\mathbb{E}\left[\nu_{N-1}\middle|x_{N-1},x_{N}\right].\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p6.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p6.3.1" class="ltx_text">Since <math id="S3.SS2.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta x^{\gamma}_{N-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>γ</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta x^{\gamma}_{N-1}</annotation></semantics></math> is measurable with respect to <math id="S3.SS2.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x_{N-1},x_{N})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x_{N-1},x_{N})</annotation></semantics></math>,</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex22" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex22.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{split}\mathbb{E}\left[\nabla_{\delta x^{\gamma}_{N-1}}\log h_{N-1}(x_{N-1})\middle|x_{N}\right]=\mathbb{E}\left[\mathbb{E}\left[\nu_{N-1}\cdot\delta x^{\gamma}_{N-1}\middle|x_{N-1},x_{N}\right]\middle|x_{N}\right]=\mathbb{E}\left[\nu_{N-1}\cdot\delta x^{\gamma}_{N-1}\middle|x_{N}\right].\end{split}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable displaystyle="true"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>[</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>γ</mi></msubsup></mrow></msub><mi>log</mi><mi>h</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em" stretchy="true">|</mo><mi>x</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>N</mi></msub><mo>]</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>[</mo><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>[</mo><mi>ν</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>δ</mi><mi>x</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>γ</mi></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em" stretchy="true">|</mo><mi>x</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>N</mi></msub><mo>]</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em" stretchy="true">|</mo><mi>x</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>N</mi></msub><mo>]</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>[</mo><mi>ν</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>δ</mi><mi>x</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>γ</mi></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em" stretchy="true">|</mo><mi>x</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>N</mi></msub><mo>]</mo><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{split}\mathbb{E}\left[\nabla_{\delta x^{\gamma}_{N-1}}\log h_{N-1}(x_{N-1})\middle|x_{N}\right]=\mathbb{E}\left[\mathbb{E}\left[\nu_{N-1}\cdot\delta x^{\gamma}_{N-1}\middle|x_{N-1},x_{N}\right]\middle|x_{N}\right]=\mathbb{E}\left[\nu_{N-1}\cdot\delta x^{\gamma}_{N-1}\middle|x_{N}\right].\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p6.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p6.4.1" class="ltx_text">By substitution,</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex23" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex23.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{split}\delta\log h^{\gamma}_{N}(x_{N})=\mathbb{E}\left[\delta\log h^{\gamma}_{N-1}-\delta\log|g^{\gamma}_{b_{N-1}*}|+\left(\nu_{N-1}-\nabla\log|g_{b_{N-1}*}|\right)\cdot\delta x^{\gamma}_{N-1}\middle|x_{N}\right].\end{split}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable displaystyle="true"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mi>log</mi><mi>h</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>γ</mi></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>N</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>N</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>[</mo><mi>δ</mi><mi>log</mi><mi>h</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>γ</mi></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>δ</mi><mi>log</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>g</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>γ</mi></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>b</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em">∗</mo></mrow></msub><mo fence="false" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo lspace="0em">+</mo><mo>(</mo><mi>ν</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>log</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>g</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>b</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em">∗</mo></mrow></msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo rspace="0.055em">)</mo><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>δ</mi><mi>x</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>γ</mi></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em" stretchy="true">|</mo><mi>x</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>N</mi></msub><mo>]</mo><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{split}\delta\log h^{\gamma}_{N}(x_{N})=\mathbb{E}\left[\delta\log h^{\gamma}_{N-1}-\delta\log|g^{\gamma}_{b_{N-1}*}|+\left(\nu_{N-1}-\nabla\log|g_{b_{N-1}*}|\right)\cdot\delta x^{\gamma}_{N-1}\middle|x_{N}\right].\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p6.5" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p6.5.1" class="ltx_text">Repeat this on <math id="S3.SS2.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta\log h^{\gamma}_{n}(x_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msubsup><mi>h</mi><mi>n</mi><mi>γ</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta\log h^{\gamma}_{n}(x_{n})</annotation></semantics></math> for each <math id="S3.SS2.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> to prove the lemma.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Note that we need the score of <math id="S3.SS2.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>h</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, or <math id="S3.SS2.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ν</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Only for large <math id="S3.SS2.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> and systems with fast decorrelation, we can use any <math id="S3.SS2.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ν</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> and the impact would be negligible.
See <a href="#S3.SS4" title="3.4. Continuous-time divergence-kernel formula for linear response ‣ 3. Derivation of the divergence-kernel formula for linear responses ‣ Divergence-Kernel method for linear responses and diffusion models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.4</span></a> for more details.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.3. </span>Preparations for SDEs</h3>

<div id="S3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This subsection introduces some notation for time-discretized SDEs and recalls some useful facts from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>]</cite> about scores for SDEs.
We want to <span id="S3.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">formally</span> pass the discrete-time results to the continuous-time limit SDE,</p>
<table id="S3.E5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(5)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{split}dx^{\gamma}_{t}=F^{\gamma}_{t}(x^{\gamma}_{t})dt+\sigma^{\gamma}_{t}(x^{\gamma}_{t})dB_{t},\end{split}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable displaystyle="true"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>γ</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>F</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>γ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>γ</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>γ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>γ</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{split}dx^{\gamma}_{t}=F^{\gamma}_{t}(x^{\gamma}_{t})dt+\sigma^{\gamma}_{t}(x^{\gamma}_{t})dB_{t},\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> denotes a standard Brownian motion.
All SDEs in this paper are in the Ito sense, so the discretized version takes the form of <a href="#S3.E6" title="In 3.3. Preparations for SDEs ‣ 3. Derivation of the divergence-kernel formula for linear responses ‣ Divergence-Kernel method for linear responses and diffusion models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Equation</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>.
Denote the density of <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> by <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Let <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> be the <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math>-algebra generated by <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{x_{\tau}\}_{\tau\leq t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{x_{\tau}\}_{\tau\leq t}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Our derivation is performed on the time span divided into small segments of length <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\Delta t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\Delta t}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Let <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> be the total number of segments, so <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N{\Delta t}=T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N{\Delta t}=T</annotation></semantics></math>.
Denote</p>
<table id="S3.Ex24" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex24.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\Delta B}_{n}:=B_{n+1}-B_{n}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\Delta B}_{n}:=B_{n+1}-B_{n}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.p2.2" class="ltx_p">Denote <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\cdot)_{n}=(\cdot)_{n{\Delta t}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\cdot)_{n}=(\cdot)_{n{\Delta t}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
The time-discretized SDE is</p>
<table id="S3.E6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(6)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x^{\gamma}_{n+1}-x^{\gamma}_{n}=F^{\gamma}(x^{\gamma}_{n}){\Delta t}+\sigma^{\gamma}(x^{\gamma}_{n}){\Delta B}_{n}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>γ</mi></msubsup><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi><mi>γ</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>F</mi><mi>γ</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi><mi>γ</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msup><mi>σ</mi><mi>γ</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi><mi>γ</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x^{\gamma}_{n+1}-x^{\gamma}_{n}=F^{\gamma}(x^{\gamma}_{n}){\Delta t}+\sigma^{\gamma}(x^{\gamma}_{n}){\Delta B}_{n}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">This section derives score formulas for time-discretized SDEs, then formally pass to the continuous-time limit.
Comparing the notations, we have</p>
<table id="S3.E7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(7)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{split}f^{\gamma}_{n}(x):=x+F^{\gamma}_{n}(x){\Delta t},\quad b_{n}:={\Delta B}_{n},\quad\\
k(b):=\left(2\pi\sqrt{\Delta t}\right)^{-M/2}\exp\left(-\frac{\left\langle b,b\right\rangle}{2{\Delta t}}\right),\quad\nabla\log k\left({\Delta B}_{n}\right)=-\frac{{\Delta B}_{n}}{{\Delta t}},\\
g^{\gamma}_{b_{n}}(x_{n}):=x_{n}+F^{\gamma}_{n}(x_{n}){\Delta t}+\sigma^{\gamma}_{n}(x_{n}){\Delta B}_{n},\\
g^{\gamma}_{b_{n}*}(x_{n})=I+\nabla F^{\gamma}_{n}(x_{n}){\Delta t}+{\Delta B}_{n}\nabla\sigma_{n}^{\gamma T}(x_{n}).\end{split}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mi>f</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>γ</mi></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>:=</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>F</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>γ</mi></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>t</mi><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mi>b</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>B</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>:=</mo><mo>(</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi><msqrt><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msqrt><mo rspace="0.167em">)</mo><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mi>exp</mi><mo>(</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo>⟨</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo>⟩</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>)</mo><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>log</mi><mi>k</mi><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>B</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>)</mo><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>g</mi><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>γ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>F</mi><mi>n</mi><mi>γ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>n</mi><mi>γ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mi>g</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>γ</mi></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>b</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em">∗</mo></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>I</mi><mo>+</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>F</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>γ</mi></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>B</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>σ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{split}f^{\gamma}_{n}(x):=x+F^{\gamma}_{n}(x){\Delta t},\quad b_{n}:={\Delta B}_{n},\quad\\
k(b):=\left(2\pi\sqrt{\Delta t}\right)^{-M/2}\exp\left(-\frac{\left\langle b,b\right\rangle}{2{\Delta t}}\right),\quad\nabla\log k\left({\Delta B}_{n}\right)=-\frac{{\Delta B}_{n}}{{\Delta t}},\\
g^{\gamma}_{b_{n}}(x_{n}):=x_{n}+F^{\gamma}_{n}(x_{n}){\Delta t}+\sigma^{\gamma}_{n}(x_{n}){\Delta B}_{n},\\
g^{\gamma}_{b_{n}*}(x_{n})=I+\nabla F^{\gamma}_{n}(x_{n}){\Delta t}+{\Delta B}_{n}\nabla\sigma_{n}^{\gamma T}(x_{n}).\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.p3.2" class="ltx_p">Here <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla F</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{\Delta B}\nabla\sigma^{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>σ</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>T</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\Delta B}\nabla\sigma^{T}</annotation></semantics></math> are matrices, whose <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>-th row <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>-th column are</p>
<table id="S3.Ex25" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex25.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{split}\left[\nabla F\right]_{ij}=\frac{\partial F^{i}}{\partial x^{j}}=:\partial_{j}F^{i},\quad\quad\left[{\Delta B}\nabla\sigma^{T}\right]_{ij}=\frac{\partial\sigma}{\partial x^{j}}{\Delta B}^{i}=:\partial_{j}\sigma{\Delta B}^{i}.\end{split}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable displaystyle="true"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mo>[</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>F</mi><mo>]</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msup><mi>F</mi><mi>i</mi></msup></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msup></mrow></mfrac><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo rspace="0.111em">:</mo><mo>∂</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>j</mi></msub><mi>F</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msup><mo rspace="3.167em">,</mo><mo>[</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>σ</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>T</mi></msup><mo>]</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>σ</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msup></mrow></mfrac><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>B</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msup><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo rspace="0.111em">:</mo><mo>∂</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>j</mi></msub><mi>σ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>B</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{split}\left[\nabla F\right]_{ij}=\frac{\partial F^{i}}{\partial x^{j}}=:\partial_{j}F^{i},\quad\quad\left[{\Delta B}\nabla\sigma^{T}\right]_{ij}=\frac{\partial\sigma}{\partial x^{j}}{\Delta B}^{i}=:\partial_{j}\sigma{\Delta B}^{i}.\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Ignoring terms that go to zero in the continuous-time limit, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>]</cite> derived explicit expressions of some terms for time-discretized SDEs,</p>
<table id="S3.E8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(8)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{split}\left|g^{\gamma}_{b_{n}*}\right|\approx 1+\sum_{i=1}^{M}\left(\partial_{i}F_{n}^{\gamma i}{\Delta t}+\partial_{i}\sigma_{n}^{\gamma}{\Delta B}^{i}\right)=1+\textnormal{div}F_{n}^{\gamma}{\Delta t}+\nabla\sigma_{n}^{\gamma}\cdot{\Delta B},\\
\textnormal{div}g_{b_{n}*}(x_{n}):=\nabla\log\left|g_{b*}\right|\approx\nabla\textnormal{div}F_{n}(x_{n}){\Delta t}+\nabla^{2}\sigma_{n}(x_{n}){\Delta B}-\nabla^{2}\sigma_{n}\nabla\sigma_{n}(x_{n}){\Delta t},\end{split}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em">|</mo><mi>g</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>γ</mi></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>b</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em">∗</mo></mrow></msub><mo fence="false">|</mo><mo lspace="0.167em">≈</mo><mn>1</mn><mo rspace="0.055em">+</mo><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>M</mi></msup><mo>(</mo><mo lspace="0em">∂</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mi>F</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>t</mi><mo rspace="0.055em">+</mo><mo>∂</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mi>σ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>γ</mi></msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>B</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msup><mo>)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mtext>div</mtext><mi>F</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>γ</mi></msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>σ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>γ</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>B</mi><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mtext>div</mtext><mi>g</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>b</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em">∗</mo></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>:=</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>log</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em">|</mo><mi>g</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0.222em">∗</mo></mrow></msub><mo fence="false">|</mo><mo lspace="0.167em">≈</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mtext>div</mtext><mi>F</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>σ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>B</mi><mo>−</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>σ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>σ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{split}\left|g^{\gamma}_{b_{n}*}\right|\approx 1+\sum_{i=1}^{M}\left(\partial_{i}F_{n}^{\gamma i}{\Delta t}+\partial_{i}\sigma_{n}^{\gamma}{\Delta B}^{i}\right)=1+\textnormal{div}F_{n}^{\gamma}{\Delta t}+\nabla\sigma_{n}^{\gamma}\cdot{\Delta B},\\
\textnormal{div}g_{b_{n}*}(x_{n}):=\nabla\log\left|g_{b*}\right|\approx\nabla\textnormal{div}F_{n}(x_{n}){\Delta t}+\nabla^{2}\sigma_{n}(x_{n}){\Delta B}-\nabla^{2}\sigma_{n}\nabla\sigma_{n}(x_{n}){\Delta t},\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.p4.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\nabla^{2}\sigma{\Delta B}=\sum_{i}\nabla\partial_{i}\sigma(x_{n}){\Delta B}_{n}^{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mo>∇</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>σ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>B</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∇</mo><msub><mo>∂</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><msubsup><mi>B</mi><mi>n</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla^{2}\sigma{\Delta B}=\sum_{i}\nabla\partial_{i}\sigma(x_{n}){\Delta B}_{n}^{i}</annotation></semantics></math>,
<math id="S3.SS3.p4.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\nabla^{2}\sigma\nabla\sigma{\Delta t}=\sum_{i}\nabla\partial_{i}\sigma(x_{n})\partial_{i}\sigma(x_{n}){\Delta t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mo>∇</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>σ</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>σ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>t</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∇</mo><msub><mo>∂</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msub><mo lspace="0em">∂</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla^{2}\sigma\nabla\sigma{\Delta t}=\sum_{i}\nabla\partial_{i}\sigma(x_{n})\partial_{i}\sigma(x_{n}){\Delta t}</annotation></semantics></math>.
This paper also needs expressions of some other terms, which will be given in <a href="#S3.SS4" title="3.4. Continuous-time divergence-kernel formula for linear response ‣ 3. Derivation of the divergence-kernel formula for linear responses ‣ Divergence-Kernel method for linear responses and diffusion models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.4</span></a>.
Then we recall the expression of the score function at <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma=0</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p5" class="ltx_para"><span id="S3.SS3.p5.1" class="ltx_ERROR undefined">{lemma}</span>
<p id="S3.SS3.p5.2" class="ltx_p">[formal divergence-kernel fomula for score of SDEs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>]</cite>] 
For the SDE in <math id="S3.SS3.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>M</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{M}</annotation></semantics></math>,</p>
<table id="S3.Ex26" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex26.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="dx_{t}=F_{t}(x_{t})dt+\sigma_{t}(x_{t})dB_{t}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">dx_{t}=F_{t}(x_{t})dt+\sigma_{t}(x_{t})dB_{t},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.p5.3" class="ltx_p">For any backward adapted process <math id="S3.SS3.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\alpha_{t}\}_{t=0}^{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\alpha_{t}\}_{t=0}^{T}</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="S3.SS3.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\nu_{t}\}_{t=0}^{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>ν</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\nu_{t}\}_{t=0}^{T}</annotation></semantics></math> be the forward covector process</p>
<table id="S3.Ex27" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex27.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{split}d\nu=\left((\nabla\sigma\nabla\sigma^{T}-\nabla F^{T}-\alpha)\nu-\nabla\textnormal{div}F+\nabla^{2}\sigma\nabla\sigma+\nabla\sigma\Delta\sigma\right)dt-(\nabla\sigma\nu^{T}+\nabla^{2}\sigma+\alpha\sigma^{-1})dB,\end{split}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable displaystyle="true"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mi>ν</mi><mo>=</mo><mo>(</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>σ</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>T</mi></msup><mo>−</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>F</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>T</mi></msup><mo>−</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mi>ν</mi><mo>−</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mtext>div</mtext><mi>F</mi><mo>+</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>σ</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo>+</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>σ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>σ</mi><mo>)</mo><mi>d</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>σ</mi><mi>ν</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>T</mi></msup><mo>+</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>σ</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>α</mi><mi>σ</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mi>d</mi><mi>B</mi><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{split}d\nu=\left((\nabla\sigma\nabla\sigma^{T}-\nabla F^{T}-\alpha)\nu-\nabla\textnormal{div}F+\nabla^{2}\sigma\nabla\sigma+\nabla\sigma\Delta\sigma\right)dt-(\nabla\sigma\nu^{T}+\nabla^{2}\sigma+\alpha\sigma^{-1})dB,\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.p5.4" class="ltx_p">with initial condition <math id="S3.SS3.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu_{0}=\nabla\log h_{0}(x_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>log</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu_{0}=\nabla\log h_{0}(x_{0})</annotation></semantics></math>,
where <math id="S3.SS3.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> is the probability density of <math id="S3.SS3.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>,
<math id="S3.SS3.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>σ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta\sigma</annotation></semantics></math> is the Laplacian of the scalar-valued function <math id="S3.SS3.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then</p>
<table id="S3.Ex28" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex28.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{split}\nabla\log h_{T}(x_{T})=\mathbb{E}\left[\nu_{T}\middle|x_{T}\right].\end{split}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable displaystyle="true"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>log</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><msub><mi>ν</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em" stretchy="true">|</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{split}\nabla\log h_{T}(x_{T})=\mathbb{E}\left[\nu_{T}\middle|x_{T}\right].\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Here, the SDE for <math id="S3.SS3.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ν</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu</annotation></semantics></math> is the limit of</p>
<table id="S3.E9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(9)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{split}\Delta\nu_{n}\approx\left((\nabla\sigma\nabla\sigma^{T}-\nabla F^{T}-\alpha_{n+1})\nu-\nabla\textnormal{div}F+\nabla^{2}\sigma\nabla\sigma+\nabla\sigma\Delta\sigma\right){\Delta t}\\
-(\nabla\sigma\nu^{T}+\nabla^{2}\sigma+\alpha_{n+1}\sigma^{-1}){\Delta B}\end{split}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>ν</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>≈</mo><mo>(</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>σ</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>T</mi></msup><mo>−</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>F</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>T</mi></msup><mo>−</mo><mi>α</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mi>ν</mi><mo>−</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mtext>div</mtext><mi>F</mi><mo>+</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>σ</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo>+</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>σ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>σ</mi><mo>)</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mo>−</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>σ</mi><mi>ν</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>T</mi></msup><mo>+</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>σ</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>α</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mi>σ</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>B</mi></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{split}\Delta\nu_{n}\approx\left((\nabla\sigma\nabla\sigma^{T}-\nabla F^{T}-\alpha_{n+1})\nu-\nabla\textnormal{div}F+\nabla^{2}\sigma\nabla\sigma+\nabla\sigma\Delta\sigma\right){\Delta t}\\
-(\nabla\sigma\nu^{T}+\nabla^{2}\sigma+\alpha_{n+1}\sigma^{-1}){\Delta B}\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.p6.2" class="ltx_p">as <math id="S3.SS3.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\Delta t}\rightarrow 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\Delta t}\rightarrow 0</annotation></semantics></math>.
Here <math id="S3.SS3.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta\nu_{n}:=\nu_{n+1}-\nu_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ν</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>ν</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta\nu_{n}:=\nu_{n+1}-\nu_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>;
<math id="S3.SS3.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{n+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>α</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{n+1}</annotation></semantics></math> is evaluated at step <math id="S3.SS3.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n+1</annotation></semantics></math>; all other terms are evaluated at step <math id="S3.SS3.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> and location <math id="S3.SS3.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.4. </span>Continuous-time divergence-kernel formula for linear response</h3>

<div id="S3.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">First, we give an expression of <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\delta\log|g^{\gamma}_{b_{n}*}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mi>log</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>g</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>γ</mi></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>b</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em">∗</mo></mrow></msub><mo fence="false" stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta\log|g^{\gamma}_{b_{n}*}|</annotation></semantics></math>, then we derive the linear response formulas.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para"><span id="S3.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_ERROR undefined">{lemma}</span>
<p id="S3.SS4.p2.2" class="ltx_p">Ignoring high-order terms which vanish as <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\Delta t}\rightarrow 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\Delta t}\rightarrow 0</annotation></semantics></math>,</p>
<table id="S3.Ex29" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex29.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{split}\delta\log|g^{\gamma}_{b_{n}*}|\approx\textnormal{div}\delta F_{n}^{\gamma}{\Delta t}+\nabla\delta\sigma_{n}^{\gamma}\cdot{\Delta B}_{n}-\nabla\sigma_{n}^{T}\nabla\delta\sigma_{n}^{\gamma}{\Delta t}.\end{split}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable displaystyle="true"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mi>log</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>g</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>γ</mi></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>b</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em">∗</mo></mrow></msub><mo fence="false" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo lspace="0.167em">≈</mo><mtext>div</mtext><mi>δ</mi><mi>F</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>γ</mi></msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>δ</mi><mi>σ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>γ</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>B</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>σ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>T</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>δ</mi><mi>σ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>γ</mi></msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{split}\delta\log|g^{\gamma}_{b_{n}*}|\approx\textnormal{div}\delta F_{n}^{\gamma}{\Delta t}+\nabla\delta\sigma_{n}^{\gamma}\cdot{\Delta B}_{n}-\nabla\sigma_{n}^{T}\nabla\delta\sigma_{n}^{\gamma}{\Delta t}.\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S3.SS4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS4.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">Substitute the expression in <a href="#S3.E8" title="In 3.3. Preparations for SDEs ‣ 3. Derivation of the divergence-kernel formula for linear responses ‣ Divergence-Kernel method for linear responses and diffusion models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Equation</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>, we get</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex30" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex30.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{split}\delta\log|g^{\gamma}_{b_{n}*}|=\frac{\delta\left|g^{\gamma}_{b*}\right|}{\left|g_{b*}\right|}=\frac{\delta\textnormal{div}F^{\gamma}{\Delta t}+\delta\nabla\sigma^{\gamma}\cdot{\Delta B}}{1+\textnormal{div}F{\Delta t}+\nabla\sigma\cdot{\Delta B}}.\end{split}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable displaystyle="true"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mi>log</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>g</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>γ</mi></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>b</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em">∗</mo></mrow></msub><mo fence="false" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo lspace="0.167em">=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em">|</mo><mi>g</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>γ</mi></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0.222em">∗</mo></mrow></msub><mo fence="false">|</mo></mrow><mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em">|</mo><mi>g</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0.222em">∗</mo></mrow></msub><mo fence="false">|</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mtext>div</mtext><mi>F</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>γ</mi></msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>σ</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>γ</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>B</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mtext>div</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>σ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{split}\delta\log|g^{\gamma}_{b_{n}*}|=\frac{\delta\left|g^{\gamma}_{b*}\right|}{\left|g_{b*}\right|}=\frac{\delta\textnormal{div}F^{\gamma}{\Delta t}+\delta\nabla\sigma^{\gamma}\cdot{\Delta B}}{1+\textnormal{div}F{\Delta t}+\nabla\sigma\cdot{\Delta B}}.\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS4.p3.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS4.p3.2.1" class="ltx_text">Recall that <math id="S3.SS4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1/(1+\varepsilon)=1-\varepsilon+O(\varepsilon^{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ε</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1/(1+\varepsilon)=1-\varepsilon+O(\varepsilon^{2})</annotation></semantics></math>, ignoring high-order terms, we get</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex31" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex31.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{split}\delta\log|g^{\gamma}_{b_{n}*}|\approx\left(\textnormal{div}\delta F^{\gamma}{\Delta t}+\nabla\delta\sigma^{\gamma}\cdot{\Delta B}\right)\left({1-\textnormal{div}F{\Delta t}-\nabla\sigma\cdot{\Delta B}}\right)\\
\approx\textnormal{div}\delta F^{\gamma}{\Delta t}+\nabla\delta\sigma^{\gamma}\cdot{\Delta B}-(\nabla\delta\sigma^{\gamma}\cdot{\Delta B})(\nabla\sigma\cdot{\Delta B})\end{split}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mi>log</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>g</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>γ</mi></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>b</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em">∗</mo></mrow></msub><mo fence="false" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo lspace="0.167em">≈</mo><mo>(</mo><mtext>div</mtext><mi>δ</mi><mi>F</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>γ</mi></msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>δ</mi><mi>σ</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>γ</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>B</mi><mo>)</mo><mo>(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mtext>div</mtext><mi>F</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>B</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mo>≈</mo><mtext>div</mtext><mi>δ</mi><mi>F</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>γ</mi></msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>δ</mi><mi>σ</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>γ</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>B</mi><mo>−</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>δ</mi><mi>σ</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>γ</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{split}\delta\log|g^{\gamma}_{b_{n}*}|\approx\left(\textnormal{div}\delta F^{\gamma}{\Delta t}+\nabla\delta\sigma^{\gamma}\cdot{\Delta B}\right)\left({1-\textnormal{div}F{\Delta t}-\nabla\sigma\cdot{\Delta B}}\right)\\
\approx\textnormal{div}\delta F^{\gamma}{\Delta t}+\nabla\delta\sigma^{\gamma}\cdot{\Delta B}-(\nabla\delta\sigma^{\gamma}\cdot{\Delta B})(\nabla\sigma\cdot{\Delta B})\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS4.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">For the last term, ignore cross terms (<math id="S3.SS4.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\Delta B}^{i}{\Delta B}^{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>B</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>B</mi><mi>j</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\Delta B}^{i}{\Delta B}^{j}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S3.SS4.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\neq j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\neq j</annotation></semantics></math>) and apply <math id="S3.SS4.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\Delta B}^{i}{\Delta B}^{i}\approx{\Delta t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>B</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>B</mi><mi>i</mi></msup></mrow><mo>≈</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\Delta B}^{i}{\Delta B}^{i}\approx{\Delta t}</annotation></semantics></math>,</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex32" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex32.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{split}(\nabla\delta\sigma^{\gamma}\cdot{\Delta B})(\nabla\sigma\cdot{\Delta B})=\sum_{i}\sum_{j}\partial_{i}\delta\sigma^{\gamma}{\Delta B}^{i}\partial_{j}\sigma{\Delta B}^{j}\approx\sum_{i}\partial_{i}\delta\sigma^{\gamma}\partial_{i}\sigma{\Delta t}=\nabla\sigma^{T}\nabla\delta\sigma^{\gamma}{\Delta t}.\end{split}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable displaystyle="true"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>δ</mi><mi>σ</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>γ</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>∂</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>σ</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>γ</mi></msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>B</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em">∂</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>j</mi></msub><mi>σ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>B</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>j</mi></msup><mo rspace="0.111em">≈</mo><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∂</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>σ</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>γ</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em">∂</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mi>σ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>σ</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>T</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>δ</mi><mi>σ</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>γ</mi></msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{split}(\nabla\delta\sigma^{\gamma}\cdot{\Delta B})(\nabla\sigma\cdot{\Delta B})=\sum_{i}\sum_{j}\partial_{i}\delta\sigma^{\gamma}{\Delta B}^{i}\partial_{j}\sigma{\Delta B}^{j}\approx\sum_{i}\partial_{i}\delta\sigma^{\gamma}\partial_{i}\sigma{\Delta t}=\nabla\sigma^{T}\nabla\delta\sigma^{\gamma}{\Delta t}.\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS4.p4.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS4.p4.2.1" class="ltx_text">Then substitute to prove the corollary.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p5" class="ltx_para"><span id="S3.SS4.p5.1" class="ltx_ERROR undefined">{lemma}</span>
<p id="S3.SS4.p5.2" class="ltx_p">[expression of <math id="S3.SS4.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta x^{\gamma}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi><mi>γ</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta x^{\gamma}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>] 
Ignoring high-order terms which vanish as <math id="S3.SS4.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\Delta t}\rightarrow 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\Delta t}\rightarrow 0</annotation></semantics></math>,</p>
<table id="S3.Ex33" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex33.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{split}\delta x^{\gamma}_{n}=-g^{-1}_{b_{n}*}\delta g^{\gamma}_{b_{n}}(x_{n})\approx-\delta F^{\gamma}_{n}{\Delta t}-\delta\sigma^{\gamma}_{n}{\Delta B}_{n}+\delta\sigma^{\gamma}_{n}\nabla\sigma_{n}{\Delta t}.\end{split}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable displaystyle="true"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mi>x</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>γ</mi></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>g</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>b</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em">∗</mo></mrow></msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>g</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>γ</mi></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo rspace="0em">≈</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>δ</mi><mi>F</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>γ</mi></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>δ</mi><mi>σ</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>γ</mi></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>B</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>δ</mi><mi>σ</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>γ</mi></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>σ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{split}\delta x^{\gamma}_{n}=-g^{-1}_{b_{n}*}\delta g^{\gamma}_{b_{n}}(x_{n})\approx-\delta F^{\gamma}_{n}{\Delta t}-\delta\sigma^{\gamma}_{n}{\Delta B}_{n}+\delta\sigma^{\gamma}_{n}\nabla\sigma_{n}{\Delta t}.\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="Thmremarkx1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmremarkx1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Remark</span></span><span id="Thmremarkx1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmremarkx1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmremarkx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This is one of the examples where some terms in the SDE setting are more explicit than discrete-time systems.
For a discrete-time system, this term would involve solving a <math id="Thmremarkx1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional linear equation system.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.3" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S3.SS4.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS4.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text">By <a href="#S3.E7" title="In 3.3. Preparations for SDEs ‣ 3. Derivation of the divergence-kernel formula for linear responses ‣ Divergence-Kernel method for linear responses and diffusion models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Equation</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>,</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex34" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex34.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{split}g^{\gamma}_{b_{n}}(x_{n}):=x_{n}+F^{\gamma}_{n}(x_{n}){\Delta t}+\sigma^{\gamma}_{n}(x_{n}){\Delta B}_{n},\\
g_{b_{n}*}(x_{n})=I+\nabla F_{n}(x_{n}){\Delta t}+{\Delta B}_{n}\nabla\sigma_{n}^{T}(x_{n}).\end{split}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>g</mi><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>γ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>F</mi><mi>n</mi><mi>γ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>n</mi><mi>γ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mi>g</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>b</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em">∗</mo></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>I</mi><mo>+</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>F</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>B</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>σ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>T</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{split}g^{\gamma}_{b_{n}}(x_{n}):=x_{n}+F^{\gamma}_{n}(x_{n}){\Delta t}+\sigma^{\gamma}_{n}(x_{n}){\Delta B}_{n},\\
g_{b_{n}*}(x_{n})=I+\nabla F_{n}(x_{n}){\Delta t}+{\Delta B}_{n}\nabla\sigma_{n}^{T}(x_{n}).\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS4.p6.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS4.p6.2.1" class="ltx_text">Hence,</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex35" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex35.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{split}\delta g^{\gamma}_{b_{n}}(x_{n})=\delta F^{\gamma}_{n}(x_{n}){\Delta t}+\delta\sigma^{\gamma}_{n}(x_{n}){\Delta B}_{n}\sim O({\Delta t}^{0.5}).\end{split}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable displaystyle="true"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>g</mi><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>γ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>F</mi><mi>n</mi><mi>γ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>n</mi><mi>γ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mn>0.5</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{split}\delta g^{\gamma}_{b_{n}}(x_{n})=\delta F^{\gamma}_{n}(x_{n}){\Delta t}+\delta\sigma^{\gamma}_{n}(x_{n}){\Delta B}_{n}\sim O({\Delta t}^{0.5}).\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS4.p6.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS4.p6.3.1" class="ltx_text">For a small matrix <math id="S3.SS4.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon\sim O({\Delta t}^{0.5})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mn>0.5</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon\sim O({\Delta t}^{0.5})</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS4.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(I+\varepsilon)^{-1}=I-\varepsilon+O({\Delta t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(I+\varepsilon)^{-1}=I-\varepsilon+O({\Delta t})</annotation></semantics></math>, so</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex36" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex36.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{split}g_{b_{n}*}^{-1}\approx I-\nabla F_{n}{\Delta t}-{\Delta B}_{n}\nabla\sigma_{n}^{T}.\end{split}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable displaystyle="true"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mi>g</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>b</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em">∗</mo></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>≈</mo><mi>I</mi><mo>−</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>F</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>B</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>σ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>T</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{split}g_{b_{n}*}^{-1}\approx I-\nabla F_{n}{\Delta t}-{\Delta B}_{n}\nabla\sigma_{n}^{T}.\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS4.p6.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS4.p6.4.1" class="ltx_text">Hence, ignoring <math id="S3.SS4.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O({\Delta t}^{1.5})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mn>1.5</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O({\Delta t}^{1.5})</annotation></semantics></math> terms which vanish as <math id="S3.SS4.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\Delta t}\rightarrow 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\Delta t}\rightarrow 0</annotation></semantics></math>,</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex37" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex37.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{split}\delta x^{\gamma}_{n}=-g_{b_{n}*}^{-1}\delta g^{\gamma}_{b_{n}}(x_{n})\approx-(I-\nabla F_{n}{\Delta t}-{\Delta B}_{n}\nabla\sigma^{T}_{n})(\delta F^{\gamma}_{n}{\Delta t}+\delta\sigma^{\gamma}_{n}{\Delta B}_{n})\\
\approx-\delta F^{\gamma}_{n}{\Delta t}-\delta\sigma^{\gamma}_{n}{\Delta B}_{n}+{\Delta B}_{n}\nabla\sigma^{T}_{n}\delta\sigma^{\gamma}_{n}{\Delta B}_{n}\end{split}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mi>x</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>γ</mi></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>g</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>b</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em">∗</mo></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mi>δ</mi><mi>g</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>γ</mi></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo rspace="0em">≈</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>I</mi><mo>−</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>F</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>B</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>σ</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>T</mi></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>δ</mi><mi>F</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>γ</mi></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>δ</mi><mi>σ</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>γ</mi></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>B</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mo rspace="0em">≈</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>δ</mi><mi>F</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>γ</mi></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>δ</mi><mi>σ</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>γ</mi></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>B</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>B</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>σ</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>T</mi></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>σ</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>γ</mi></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>B</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{split}\delta x^{\gamma}_{n}=-g_{b_{n}*}^{-1}\delta g^{\gamma}_{b_{n}}(x_{n})\approx-(I-\nabla F_{n}{\Delta t}-{\Delta B}_{n}\nabla\sigma^{T}_{n})(\delta F^{\gamma}_{n}{\Delta t}+\delta\sigma^{\gamma}_{n}{\Delta B}_{n})\\
\approx-\delta F^{\gamma}_{n}{\Delta t}-\delta\sigma^{\gamma}_{n}{\Delta B}_{n}+{\Delta B}_{n}\nabla\sigma^{T}_{n}\delta\sigma^{\gamma}_{n}{\Delta B}_{n}\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS4.p6.5" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS4.p6.5.1" class="ltx_text">Note that <math id="S3.SS4.p6.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\nabla\sigma^{T}_{n}{\Delta B}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>σ</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>T</mi></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>B</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla\sigma^{T}_{n}{\Delta B}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> is a number, and ignoring the cross terms, we get</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex38" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex38.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{split}{\Delta B}_{n}(\nabla\sigma^{T}_{n}{\Delta B}_{n})={\Delta B}_{n}({\Delta B}_{n}^{T}\nabla\sigma_{n})\approx\nabla\sigma_{n}{\Delta t}.\end{split}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable displaystyle="true"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>B</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>σ</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>T</mi></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>B</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>B</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>B</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>T</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>σ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>≈</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>σ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{split}{\Delta B}_{n}(\nabla\sigma^{T}_{n}{\Delta B}_{n})={\Delta B}_{n}({\Delta B}_{n}^{T}\nabla\sigma_{n})\approx\nabla\sigma_{n}{\Delta t}.\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS4.p6.6" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS4.p6.6.1" class="ltx_text">Then substitute to prove the lemma.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p7" class="ltx_para"><span id="S3.SS4.p7.1" class="ltx_ERROR undefined">{lemma}</span>
<p id="S3.SS4.p7.2" class="ltx_p">Ignoring high-order terms which vanish as <math id="S3.SS4.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\Delta t}\rightarrow 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\Delta t}\rightarrow 0</annotation></semantics></math>,
let <math id="S3.SS4.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>σ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta\sigma</annotation></semantics></math> denote the Laplacian of <math id="S3.SS4.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math>,
then</p>
<table id="S3.Ex39" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex39.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{split}\nabla\log\left|g_{b_{n}*}\right|\cdot\delta x^{\gamma}_{n}\approx-\delta\sigma^{\gamma}_{n}\Delta\sigma_{n}{\Delta t}.\end{split}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable displaystyle="true"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>log</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em">|</mo><mi>g</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>b</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em">∗</mo></mrow></msub><mo fence="false">|</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>δ</mi><mi>x</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>γ</mi></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub><mo rspace="0em">≈</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>δ</mi><mi>σ</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>γ</mi></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>σ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{split}\nabla\log\left|g_{b_{n}*}\right|\cdot\delta x^{\gamma}_{n}\approx-\delta\sigma^{\gamma}_{n}\Delta\sigma_{n}{\Delta t}.\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.4" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S3.SS4.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p8.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS4.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text">By <a href="#S3.E8" title="In 3.3. Preparations for SDEs ‣ 3. Derivation of the divergence-kernel formula for linear responses ‣ Divergence-Kernel method for linear responses and diffusion models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Equation</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a> and <a href="#S3.SS4" title="3.4. Continuous-time divergence-kernel formula for linear response ‣ 3. Derivation of the divergence-kernel formula for linear responses ‣ Divergence-Kernel method for linear responses and diffusion models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.4</span></a>,</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex40" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex40.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{split}\nabla\log\left|g_{b_{n}*}\right|\approx\nabla\textnormal{div}F_{n}(x_{n}){\Delta t}+\nabla^{2}\sigma_{n}(x_{n}){\Delta B}_{n}-\nabla^{2}\sigma_{n}\nabla\sigma_{n}(x_{n}){\Delta t},\\
\delta x^{\gamma}_{n}=-g^{-1}_{b_{n}*}\delta g^{\gamma}_{b_{n}}(x_{n})\approx-\delta F^{\gamma}_{n}{\Delta t}-\delta\sigma^{\gamma}_{n}{\Delta B}_{n}+\delta\sigma^{\gamma}_{n}\nabla\sigma^{T}_{n}{\Delta t}.\end{split}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>log</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em">|</mo><mi>g</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>b</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em">∗</mo></mrow></msub><mo fence="false">|</mo><mo lspace="0.167em">≈</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mtext>div</mtext><mi>F</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>σ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>B</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>σ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>σ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mi>x</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>γ</mi></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>g</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>b</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em">∗</mo></mrow></msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>g</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>γ</mi></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo rspace="0em">≈</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>δ</mi><mi>F</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>γ</mi></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>δ</mi><mi>σ</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>γ</mi></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>B</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>δ</mi><mi>σ</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>γ</mi></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>σ</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>T</mi></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{split}\nabla\log\left|g_{b_{n}*}\right|\approx\nabla\textnormal{div}F_{n}(x_{n}){\Delta t}+\nabla^{2}\sigma_{n}(x_{n}){\Delta B}_{n}-\nabla^{2}\sigma_{n}\nabla\sigma_{n}(x_{n}){\Delta t},\\
\delta x^{\gamma}_{n}=-g^{-1}_{b_{n}*}\delta g^{\gamma}_{b_{n}}(x_{n})\approx-\delta F^{\gamma}_{n}{\Delta t}-\delta\sigma^{\gamma}_{n}{\Delta B}_{n}+\delta\sigma^{\gamma}_{n}\nabla\sigma^{T}_{n}{\Delta t}.\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS4.p8.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS4.p8.2.1" class="ltx_text">In the product, the only term smaller than or equal to <math id="S3.SS4.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O({\Delta t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O({\Delta t})</annotation></semantics></math> is</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex41" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex41.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{split}\nabla\log\left|g_{b_{n}*}\right|\cdot\delta x^{\gamma}_{n}\approx\nabla^{2}\sigma_{n}(x_{n}){\Delta B}_{n}\cdot-\delta\sigma^{\gamma}_{n}{\Delta B}_{n}\end{split}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable displaystyle="true"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>log</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em">|</mo><mi>g</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>b</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em">∗</mo></mrow></msub><mo fence="false">|</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>δ</mi><mi>x</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>γ</mi></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>≈</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>σ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>B</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>δ</mi><mi>σ</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>γ</mi></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>B</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{split}\nabla\log\left|g_{b_{n}*}\right|\cdot\delta x^{\gamma}_{n}\approx\nabla^{2}\sigma_{n}(x_{n}){\Delta B}_{n}\cdot-\delta\sigma^{\gamma}_{n}{\Delta B}_{n}\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS4.p8.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS4.p8.3.1" class="ltx_text">Ignore the cross term, we get</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex42" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex42.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{split}\nabla^{2}\sigma{\Delta B}\cdot{\Delta B}=\sum_{i,j}\partial_{i}\partial_{j}\sigma{\Delta B}^{j}{\Delta B}^{i}\approx\sum_{i}\partial_{i}^{2}\sigma{\Delta t}=:\Delta\sigma{\Delta t},\end{split}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable displaystyle="true"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mo>∇</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>σ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><mrow><msub><mo lspace="0em">∂</mo><mi>j</mi></msub><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>B</mi><mi>j</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>B</mi><mi>i</mi></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">≈</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mi>i</mi></munder><mrow><msubsup><mo>∂</mo><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{split}\nabla^{2}\sigma{\Delta B}\cdot{\Delta B}=\sum_{i,j}\partial_{i}\partial_{j}\sigma{\Delta B}^{j}{\Delta B}^{i}\approx\sum_{i}\partial_{i}^{2}\sigma{\Delta t}=:\Delta\sigma{\Delta t},\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS4.p8.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS4.p8.4.1" class="ltx_text">Then substitute to prove the lemma.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p9.1" class="ltx_p">With these, we can formally pass <a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2. Discrete-time divergence-kernel formula for linear responses ‣ 3. Derivation of the divergence-kernel formula for linear responses ‣ Divergence-Kernel method for linear responses and diffusion models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a> to the continuous-time limit.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p10.1" class="ltx_p">See <a href="#S1.SS1" title="1.1. Main results ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Divergence-Kernel method for linear responses and diffusion models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.1</span></a></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.5" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S3.SS4.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p11.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS4.p11.1.1" class="ltx_text">Substitute <a href="#S3.SS4" title="3.4. Continuous-time divergence-kernel formula for linear response ‣ 3. Derivation of the divergence-kernel formula for linear responses ‣ Divergence-Kernel method for linear responses and diffusion models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Sections</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.4</span></a>, <a href="#S3.SS4" title="3.4. Continuous-time divergence-kernel formula for linear response ‣ 3. Derivation of the divergence-kernel formula for linear responses ‣ Divergence-Kernel method for linear responses and diffusion models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.4</span></a> and <a href="#S3.SS4" title="3.4. Continuous-time divergence-kernel formula for linear response ‣ 3. Derivation of the divergence-kernel formula for linear responses ‣ Divergence-Kernel method for linear responses and diffusion models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.4</span></a> into <a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2. Discrete-time divergence-kernel formula for linear responses ‣ 3. Derivation of the divergence-kernel formula for linear responses ‣ Divergence-Kernel method for linear responses and diffusion models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>, we get</span></p>
<table id="S3.E10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(10)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{split}\delta\log h^{\gamma}_{N}(x_{N})=\mathbb{E}\left[\delta\log h^{\gamma}_{0}+\sum_{n=0}^{N-1}\nu_{n}\cdot\delta x^{\gamma}_{n}-\nabla\log|g_{b_{n}*}|\cdot\delta x^{\gamma}_{n}-\delta\log|g^{\gamma}_{b_{n}*}|\middle|x_{N}\right]\\
\approx\mathbb{E}\left[\delta\log h^{\gamma}_{0}+\sum_{n=0}^{N-1}\nu_{n}\cdot(-\delta F^{\gamma}_{n}{\Delta t}-\delta\sigma^{\gamma}_{n}{\Delta B}_{n}+\delta\sigma^{\gamma}_{n}\nabla\sigma_{n}{\Delta t})\right.\\
\left.+\delta\sigma^{\gamma}_{n}\Delta\sigma_{n}{\Delta t}-\textnormal{div}\delta F_{n}^{\gamma}{\Delta t}-\nabla\delta\sigma_{n}^{\gamma}\cdot{\Delta B}_{n}+\nabla\sigma_{n}^{T}\nabla\delta\sigma_{n}^{\gamma}{\Delta t}\middle|x_{N}\right].\end{split}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mi>log</mi><mi>h</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>γ</mi></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>N</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>N</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>[</mo><mi>δ</mi><mi>log</mi><mi>h</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>γ</mi></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>0</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.055em">+</mo><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mi>ν</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>δ</mi><mi>x</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>γ</mi></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>log</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>g</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>b</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em">∗</mo></mrow></msub><mo fence="false" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>δ</mi><mi>x</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>γ</mi></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>δ</mi><mi>log</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>g</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>γ</mi></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>b</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em">∗</mo></mrow></msub><mo fence="false" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em" stretchy="true">|</mo><mi>x</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>N</mi></msub><mo>]</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mo>≈</mo><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>[</mo><mi>δ</mi><mi>log</mi><mi>h</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>γ</mi></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>0</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.055em">+</mo><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mi>ν</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>δ</mi><mi>F</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>γ</mi></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>δ</mi><mi>σ</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>γ</mi></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>B</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>δ</mi><mi>σ</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>γ</mi></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>σ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>δ</mi><mi>σ</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>γ</mi></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>σ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mtext>div</mtext><mi>δ</mi><mi>F</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>γ</mi></msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>δ</mi><mi>σ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>γ</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>B</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>σ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>T</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>δ</mi><mi>σ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>γ</mi></msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em" stretchy="true">|</mo><mi>x</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>N</mi></msub><mo>]</mo><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{split}\delta\log h^{\gamma}_{N}(x_{N})=\mathbb{E}\left[\delta\log h^{\gamma}_{0}+\sum_{n=0}^{N-1}\nu_{n}\cdot\delta x^{\gamma}_{n}-\nabla\log|g_{b_{n}*}|\cdot\delta x^{\gamma}_{n}-\delta\log|g^{\gamma}_{b_{n}*}|\middle|x_{N}\right]\\
\approx\mathbb{E}\left[\delta\log h^{\gamma}_{0}+\sum_{n=0}^{N-1}\nu_{n}\cdot(-\delta F^{\gamma}_{n}{\Delta t}-\delta\sigma^{\gamma}_{n}{\Delta B}_{n}+\delta\sigma^{\gamma}_{n}\nabla\sigma_{n}{\Delta t})\right.\\
\left.+\delta\sigma^{\gamma}_{n}\Delta\sigma_{n}{\Delta t}-\textnormal{div}\delta F_{n}^{\gamma}{\Delta t}-\nabla\delta\sigma_{n}^{\gamma}\cdot{\Delta B}_{n}+\nabla\sigma_{n}^{T}\nabla\delta\sigma_{n}^{\gamma}{\Delta t}\middle|x_{N}\right].\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS4.p11.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS4.p11.2.1" class="ltx_text">Then formally pass to the limit <math id="S3.SS4.p11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\Delta t}\rightarrow 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\Delta t}\rightarrow 0</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p12" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p12.1" class="ltx_p">We also formally pass to the infinite-time limit of stationary distributions <math id="S3.SS4.p12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h^{\gamma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>h</mi><mi>γ</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{\gamma}</annotation></semantics></math>.
We need to further assume that <math id="S3.SS4.p12.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS4.p12.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> are stationary, and that there is <math id="S3.SS4.p12.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S3.SS4.p12.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> only depends on <math id="S3.SS4.p12.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{t}\sim x_{T^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{t}\sim x_{T^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math>, so <math id="S3.SS4.p12.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\alpha,\nu,x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ν</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\alpha,\nu,x)</annotation></semantics></math> are jointly stationary.
<span id="S3.SS4.p12.1.1" class="ltx_ERROR undefined">{corollary}</span> [formal ergodic divergence-kernel linear response] 
For any <math id="S3.SS4.p12.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> observable function <math id="S3.SS4.p12.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi:\mathbb{R}^{M}\rightarrow R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>M</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi:\mathbb{R}^{M}\rightarrow R</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="S3.SS4.p12.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h^{\gamma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>h</mi><mi>γ</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{\gamma}</annotation></semantics></math> be the stationary measure under parameter <math id="S3.SS4.p12.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math>, then the linear response of the averaged observable can be expressed almost surely on one path:</p>
<table id="S3.Ex43" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex43.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{split}\delta\int\Phi(x)h^{\gamma}(x)dx\overset{a.s.}{=}\lim_{W\rightarrow\infty}\lim_{T\rightarrow\infty}\frac{1}{T}\int_{t=0}^{T}\left(\int_{\tau=0}^{W}(\Phi(x_{t+\tau})-\Phi_{avg})d\tau\right)(\delta\sigma_{t}^{\gamma}\nabla\sigma_{t}^{T}\nu\\
-\delta F_{t}^{\gamma T}\nu-\textnormal{div}\delta F_{t}^{\gamma}+\delta\sigma_{t}^{\gamma}\Delta\sigma_{t}+\nabla\sigma_{t}^{T}\nabla\delta\sigma_{t}^{\gamma})dt-(\delta\sigma_{t}^{\gamma}\nu^{T}+\nabla\delta\sigma_{t}^{\gamma T})dB_{t},\\
\textnormal{where}\quad\Phi_{avg}:=\int\Phi(x)h(x)\overset{a.s.}{=}\lim_{T\rightarrow\infty}\frac{1}{T}\int_{t=0}^{T}\Phi(x_{t})dt.\\
\end{split}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>∫</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mi>h</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>γ</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mi>d</mi><mi>x</mi><mover accent="true"><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">a</mi><mo lspace="0em" mathsize="0.700em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">s</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" mathsize="0.700em">.</mo></mrow></mover><mo lspace="0.1389em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.0835em">lim</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.0835em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">lim</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow></msub><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>T</mi></mfrac><mo rspace="0em">∫</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>T</mi></msup><mo>(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∫</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>W</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mi>d</mi><mi>τ</mi><mo>)</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>δ</mi><mi>σ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>γ</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>σ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>T</mi></msup><mi>ν</mi></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>δ</mi><mi>F</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msup><mi>ν</mi><mo>−</mo><mtext>div</mtext><mi>δ</mi><mi>F</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>γ</mi></msup><mo>+</mo><mi>δ</mi><mi>σ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>γ</mi></msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>σ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>σ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>T</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>δ</mi><mi>σ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>γ</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mi>d</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>δ</mi><mi>σ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>γ</mi></msup><mi>ν</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>T</mi></msup><mo>+</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>δ</mi><mi>σ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mi>d</mi><mi>B</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mrow><mtext>where</mtext><mspace style="width:2.000em;" width="2.000em"></mspace><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">:=</mo><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">a</mi><mo lspace="0em" mathsize="0.700em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">s</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" mathsize="0.700em">.</mo></mrow></mover><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">lim</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>T</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∫</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></msubsup><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">d</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{split}\delta\int\Phi(x)h^{\gamma}(x)dx\overset{a.s.}{=}\lim_{W\rightarrow\infty}\lim_{T\rightarrow\infty}\frac{1}{T}\int_{t=0}^{T}\left(\int_{\tau=0}^{W}(\Phi(x_{t+\tau})-\Phi_{avg})d\tau\right)(\delta\sigma_{t}^{\gamma}\nabla\sigma_{t}^{T}\nu\\
-\delta F_{t}^{\gamma T}\nu-\textnormal{div}\delta F_{t}^{\gamma}+\delta\sigma_{t}^{\gamma}\Delta\sigma_{t}+\nabla\sigma_{t}^{T}\nabla\delta\sigma_{t}^{\gamma})dt-(\delta\sigma_{t}^{\gamma}\nu^{T}+\nabla\delta\sigma_{t}^{\gamma T})dB_{t},\\
\textnormal{where}\quad\Phi_{avg}:=\int\Phi(x)h(x)\overset{a.s.}{=}\lim_{T\rightarrow\infty}\frac{1}{T}\int_{t=0}^{T}\Phi(x_{t})dt.\\
\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p13" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p13.1" class="ltx_p">The discrete-time expression is</p>
<table id="S3.Ex44" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex44.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{split}\delta\int\Phi(x)h^{\gamma}(x)dx\overset{a.s.}{=}\lim_{W\rightarrow\infty}\lim_{N\rightarrow\infty}\frac{1}{N}\sum_{n=0}^{N}\left(\sum_{m=1}^{W}(\Phi(x_{n+m})-\Phi_{avg})\right)\nu_{n}\cdot\delta\sigma^{\gamma}_{n}\nabla\sigma_{n}{\Delta t}\\
-\nu_{n}\cdot(\delta F^{\gamma}_{n}{\Delta t}+\delta\sigma^{\gamma}_{n}{\Delta B}_{n})+\delta\sigma^{\gamma}_{n}\Delta\sigma_{n}{\Delta t}-\textnormal{div}\delta F_{n}^{\gamma}{\Delta t}-\nabla\delta\sigma_{n}^{\gamma}\cdot{\Delta B}_{n}+\nabla\sigma_{n}^{T}\nabla\delta\sigma_{n}^{\gamma}{\Delta t}.\end{split}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>∫</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mi>h</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>γ</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mi>d</mi><mi>x</mi><mover accent="true"><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">a</mi><mo lspace="0em" mathsize="0.700em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">s</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" mathsize="0.700em">.</mo></mrow></mover><mo lspace="0.1389em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.0835em">lim</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.0835em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">lim</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow></msub><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>N</mi></mfrac><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>N</mi></msup><mo>(</mo><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>W</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>)</mo><mi>ν</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>δ</mi><mi>σ</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>γ</mi></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>σ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>ν</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>δ</mi><mi>F</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>γ</mi></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>δ</mi><mi>σ</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>γ</mi></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>B</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>+</mo><mi>δ</mi><mi>σ</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>γ</mi></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>σ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mtext>div</mtext><mi>δ</mi><mi>F</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>γ</mi></msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>δ</mi><mi>σ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>γ</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>B</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>σ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>T</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>δ</mi><mi>σ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>γ</mi></msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{split}\delta\int\Phi(x)h^{\gamma}(x)dx\overset{a.s.}{=}\lim_{W\rightarrow\infty}\lim_{N\rightarrow\infty}\frac{1}{N}\sum_{n=0}^{N}\left(\sum_{m=1}^{W}(\Phi(x_{n+m})-\Phi_{avg})\right)\nu_{n}\cdot\delta\sigma^{\gamma}_{n}\nabla\sigma_{n}{\Delta t}\\
-\nu_{n}\cdot(\delta F^{\gamma}_{n}{\Delta t}+\delta\sigma^{\gamma}_{n}{\Delta B}_{n})+\delta\sigma^{\gamma}_{n}\Delta\sigma_{n}{\Delta t}-\textnormal{div}\delta F_{n}^{\gamma}{\Delta t}-\nabla\delta\sigma_{n}^{\gamma}\cdot{\Delta B}_{n}+\nabla\sigma_{n}^{T}\nabla\delta\sigma_{n}^{\gamma}{\Delta t}.\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.5. </span>Discussions</h3>

<div id="S3.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The discussions in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>]</cite> are still valid.
Roughly speaking, we want <math id="S3.SS5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> larger than the largest contraction rate.
Also, the divergence-kernel method does not work well in highly contracting systems with little to no noise, the case where the path-perturbation method is good.
We may need to use all three methods (path, kernel, and divergence) together to obtain an ultimate solution, as proposed in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Compared to the path-kernel method in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>, <a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>]</cite>, the divergence-kernel method has the following advantages:</p>
<ol id="S3.I1" class="ltx_enumerate" style="--ltx-enum-leftmargin:0pt;">
<li id="S3.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1)</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">It involves only forward processes, so the memory cost is much smaller.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(2)</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Its cost is almost independent of <span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">both</span> the number of observables <span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">and</span> the parameters.
In contrast, the cost of the path-kernel method is independent of <span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">either</span> the number of observables <span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">or</span> the parameters.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
<p id="S3.SS5.p2.2" class="ltx_p">It also has some disadvantages:</p>
<ol id="S3.I2" class="ltx_enumerate" style="--ltx-enum-leftmargin:0pt;">
<li id="S3.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1)</span> 
<div id="S3.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Its computational complexity per step is higher than that for the path-kernel method, although still on the same order as that for the path-kernel method. This is because it involves the second-order derivative.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(2)</span> 
<div id="S3.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For dynamical systems generated from discretized PDEs, typically <math id="S3.I2.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\lambda_{min}|&gt;|\lambda_{max}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\lambda_{min}|&gt;|\lambda_{max}|</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.I2.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{max}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{max}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.I2.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{min}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{min}</annotation></semantics></math> are the largest and the smallest (most negative) Lyapunov exponents; hence, the path-kernel method requires a smaller <math id="S3.I2.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>, so the estimator has smaller size and the average converges faster.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4. </span>Numerical examples</h2>

<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1. </span>1-dimensional process with multiplicative noise</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We use <a href="#S1.SS1" title="1.1. Main results ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Divergence-Kernel method for linear responses and diffusion models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.1</span></a> to compute the linear response of the one-dimensional process with multiplicative noise. The SDE is</p>
<table id="S4.Ex45" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex45.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{split}dx=\beta_{t}(\gamma-x)dt+\sqrt{\beta_{t}}(0.5+\exp\left((\gamma-x)^{2}\right)dB,\quad\textnormal{where}\quad\beta_{t}:=1+3t.\end{split}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable displaystyle="true"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>β</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mi>d</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><msqrt><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msqrt><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0.5</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>exp</mi><mo>(</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>)</mo><mi>d</mi><mi>B</mi><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mtext>where</mtext><mi>β</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{split}dx=\beta_{t}(\gamma-x)dt+\sqrt{\beta_{t}}(0.5+\exp\left((\gamma-x)^{2}\right)dB,\quad\textnormal{where}\quad\beta_{t}:=1+3t.\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">The initial distribution is <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{0}\sim N(0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{0}\sim N(0,1)</annotation></semantics></math>.
Set <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T=0.1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T=0.1</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\Delta t}=0.01" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0.01</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\Delta t}=0.01</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=10</annotation></semantics></math>.
Note that the problem is symmetric for <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We are only interested in terminal conditions <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{T}</annotation></semantics></math> in the interval <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[-2,2]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[-2,2]</annotation></semantics></math>, which is further partitioned into <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>10</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10</annotation></semantics></math> subintervals.
We simulate many sample paths, then count the number of paths whose <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{T}</annotation></semantics></math> fall into a specific subinterval, to obtain the empirical distribution of <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{T}</annotation></semantics></math> on subintervals.
Then we compute the linear response <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta\log h_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta\log h_{T}</annotation></semantics></math> by averaging the divergence-kernel formula over these paths, and see if our results reflect the trend of <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\log h_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\log h_{T}</annotation></semantics></math> from the empirical distribution.
We also use the average of <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ν</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu_{T}</annotation></semantics></math> to compute the scores <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla\log h_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>log</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla\log h_{T}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Each subinterval contains at least 180 samples.
The <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta\log h_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta\log h_{T}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla\log h_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>log</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla\log h_{T}</annotation></semantics></math> computed for different <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>’s are shown in <a href="#S4.F1" title="In 4.1. 1-dimensional process with multiplicative noise ‣ 4. Numerical examples ‣ Divergence-Kernel method for linear responses and diffusion models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.
Both correctly reflect the trend of <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\log h_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\log h_{T}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F1" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2"><img src="2509.03992v1/figs/1d_lines_lr.png" id="S4.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_figure_panel ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:210/197;" width="210" height="197" alt="Refer to caption"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2"><img src="2509.03992v1/figs/1d_lines_score.png" id="S4.F1.g2" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_figure_panel ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:215/198;" width="215" height="198" alt="Refer to caption"></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 1</span>. </span><span id="S4.F1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">
Divergence-kernel method for the linear responses and scores of the 1-dimensional SDE with multiplicative noise, <math id="S4.F1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T=0.3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T=0.3</annotation></semantics></math>.
The dots are <math id="S4.F1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\log h_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\log h_{T}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Each short line is a linear response or score computed by the divergence-kernel algorithm, averaged on paths whose terminal conditions <math id="S4.F1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{T}</annotation></semantics></math> fall into the same subinterval.
Left: linear responses <math id="S4.F1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta\log h_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta\log h_{T}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Right: scores <math id="S4.F1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla\log h_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>log</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla\log h_{T}</annotation></semantics></math> .
</span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2. </span>40-dimensional Lorenz 96 system with multiplicative noise</h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We use <a href="#S3.SS4" title="3.4. Continuous-time divergence-kernel formula for linear response ‣ 3. Derivation of the divergence-kernel formula for linear responses ‣ Divergence-Kernel method for linear responses and diffusion models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.4</span></a> to compute the linear response of the stationary density of the Lorenz 96 model <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite>.
The dimension of the system is <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M=40" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>40</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M=40</annotation></semantics></math>.
The SDE is</p>
<table id="S4.EGx3" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_eqnarray ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.Ex46"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex46.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle dx^{i}=\left(\left(x^{i+1}-x^{i-2}\right)x^{i-1}-x^{i}+8+\gamma-0.01(x^{i})^{2}\right)dt+0.5+\exp\left((x-\gamma)^{2}\right)dB^{i}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>8</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>0.01</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>0.5</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>B</mi><mi>i</mi></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle dx^{i}=\left(\left(x^{i+1}-x^{i-2}\right)x^{i-1}-x^{i}+8+\gamma-0.01(x^{i})^{2}\right)dt+0.5+\exp\left((x-\gamma)^{2}\right)dB^{i},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.Ex47"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex47.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\textnormal{where}\quad i=1,\ldots,M;\quad x_{0}\sim{\mathcal{N}}(0,I)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mtext>where</mtext><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>M</mi><mo rspace="1.167em">;</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\textnormal{where}\quad i=1,\ldots,M;\quad x_{0}\sim{\mathcal{N}}(0,I).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Here <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> labels different directions in <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>M</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{M}</annotation></semantics></math>, and it is assumed that <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x^{-1}=x^{M-1},x^{0}=x^{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>M</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x^{-1}=x^{M-1},x^{0}=x^{M}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x^{M+1}=x^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x^{M+1}=x^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>.
We added noise and the <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-0.01(x^{i})^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>0.01</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-0.01(x^{i})^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> term, which prevents the noise from carrying us to infinitely far away.
We choose the observable function to be</p>
<table id="S4.Ex48" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex48.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi(x):=\frac{1}{M}|x|^{2}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>M</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi(x):=\frac{1}{M}|x|^{2}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We use the Euler integration scheme with <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\Delta t}=0.002" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0.002</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\Delta t}=0.002</annotation></semantics></math>.
A typical orbit is in <a href="#S4.F2" title="In 4.2. 40-dimensional Lorenz 96 system with multiplicative noise ‣ 4. Numerical examples ‣ Divergence-Kernel method for linear responses and diffusion models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>.
Then we use the ergodic linear response formula <a href="#S3.SS4" title="3.4. Continuous-time divergence-kernel formula for linear response ‣ 3. Derivation of the divergence-kernel formula for linear responses ‣ Divergence-Kernel method for linear responses and diffusion models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.4</span></a> to compute the linear response of the observable averaged on the stationary measure.
The averaged observable <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi_{avg}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi_{avg}</annotation></semantics></math> and the linear response <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta\int\Phi h^{\gamma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>h</mi><mi>γ</mi></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta\int\Phi h^{\gamma}</annotation></semantics></math> are averaged over 7 orbits of length <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T=400" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>400</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T=400</annotation></semantics></math>.
The decorrelation time is <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W=1.5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1.5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W=1.5</annotation></semantics></math>.
The results shown in <a href="#S4.F2" title="In 4.2. 40-dimensional Lorenz 96 system with multiplicative noise ‣ 4. Numerical examples ‣ Divergence-Kernel method for linear responses and diffusion models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>; the linear response computed using the divergence-kernel method is correct.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F2" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2"><img src="2509.03992v1/figs/lorenz_orbit.png" id="S4.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_figure_panel ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:204/205;" width="204" height="205" alt="Refer to caption"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2"><img src="2509.03992v1/figs/lorenz_lr.png" id="S4.F2.g2" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_figure_panel ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:214/205;" width="214" height="205" alt="Refer to caption"></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 2</span>. </span><span id="S4.F2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">
Left: plot of <math id="S4.F2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x^{0}_{t},x^{1}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi><mn>0</mn></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x^{0}_{t},x^{1}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> from a typical orbit of time length <math id="S4.F2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1.5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1.5</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1.5</annotation></semantics></math>.
Right: linear response computed by divergence-kernel method.
</span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5. </span>A simple forward-only diffusion model</h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We consider the problem in which we are given <math id="S5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{data}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{data}</annotation></semantics></math> many samples <math id="S5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{k}\in\mathbb{R}^{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>M</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{k}\in\mathbb{R}^{M}</annotation></semantics></math>, whose true distribution is unknown.
Then we use gradient descent to find an SDE such that it maps the Gaussian distribution to match the data.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">First, we describe how to generate samples.
Note that the information in this paragraph will not be available when we infer the SDE.
The samples are generated by the SDE</p>
<table id="S5.Ex49" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex49.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{split}dx_{t}=F^{\gamma_{true}}_{t}(x_{t})dt+\sigma^{\gamma_{true}}_{t}(x_{t})dB_{t},\end{split}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable displaystyle="true"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>F</mi><mi>t</mi><msub><mi>γ</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>t</mi><msub><mi>γ</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{split}dx_{t}=F^{\gamma_{true}}_{t}(x_{t})dt+\sigma^{\gamma_{true}}_{t}(x_{t})dB_{t},\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where the true parameters <math id="S5.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{true}:=[\gamma_{true}^{0},\ldots,\gamma_{true}^{N_{\gamma}}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>γ</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><mn>0</mn></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>γ</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>γ</mi></msub></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{true}:=[\gamma_{true}^{0},\ldots,\gamma_{true}^{N_{\gamma}}]</annotation></semantics></math>.
The samples are <math id="S5.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y:=x_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>:=</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y:=x_{T}</annotation></semantics></math> obtained at the end of independent paths.
Denote the empirical measure, which we try to match, as</p>
<table id="S5.Ex50" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex50.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{split}P(\cdot):=\frac{1}{N_{data}}\sum_{k=1}^{N_{data}}\delta_{y_{k}}(\cdot).\end{split}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable displaystyle="true"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msub><mi>N</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow></msub></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow></msub></munderover><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{split}P(\cdot):=\frac{1}{N_{data}}\sum_{k=1}^{N_{data}}\delta_{y_{k}}(\cdot).\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.p2.3" class="ltx_p">Our framework can work on more general data, but here we restrict ourselves to the simple case, so that after optimization, we can check our result with the true parameters.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We want to fit several unknown parameters <math id="S5.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma=[\gamma^{0},\ldots,\gamma^{N_{\gamma}}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msup><mi>γ</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>γ</mi><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>γ</mi></msub></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma=[\gamma^{0},\ldots,\gamma^{N_{\gamma}}]</annotation></semantics></math> in an SDE such that its marginal distribution matches the data.
The prototype of the SDE is</p>
<table id="S5.Ex51" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex51.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{split}dx_{t}=F^{\gamma}_{t}(x_{t})dt+\sigma^{\gamma}_{t}(x_{t})dB_{t}.\end{split}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable displaystyle="true"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>F</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>γ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>γ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{split}dx_{t}=F^{\gamma}_{t}(x_{t})dt+\sigma^{\gamma}_{t}(x_{t})dB_{t}.\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.p3.2" class="ltx_p">Denote the density at <math id="S5.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> by <math id="S5.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h^{\gamma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>h</mi><mi>γ</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{\gamma}</annotation></semantics></math> and measure by <math id="S5.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{\gamma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>γ</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{\gamma}</annotation></semantics></math> – this is the distribution that we want to optimize.
For each <math id="S5.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math> during optimization, we simulate <math id="S5.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>-many paths by this SDE, and we label each path by <math id="S5.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>l</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l</annotation></semantics></math>, in particular, the terminal location is denoted by <math id="S5.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{T,l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{T,l}</annotation></semantics></math>.
In future research, we can seek to use more flexible prototypes, such as neural networks.
However, note that the gradient used in the optimization is <span id="S5.p3.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">not</span> computed by backpropagation; rather, it only involves a covector process that runs forward in time and costs much less memory.
We also remark that we can choose to optimize the stationary distribution using <a href="#S3.SS4" title="3.4. Continuous-time divergence-kernel formula for linear response ‣ 3. Derivation of the divergence-kernel formula for linear responses ‣ Divergence-Kernel method for linear responses and diffusion models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.4</span></a>, which may be more efficient for some cases.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The difference between the generated distribution and the data is measured by the KL-divergence</p>
<table id="S5.Ex52" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex52.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{split}D_{\text{KL}}(P\parallel H^{\gamma}):=\int_{y\in{\mathbb{R}^{M}}}\log{\frac{P(dy)}{H^{\gamma}(dy)}}\,P(dy).\end{split}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable displaystyle="true"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mi>D</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mtext>KL</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><mi>H</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>γ</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo rspace="0.111em">:=</mo><mo>∫</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>M</mi></msup></mrow></msub><mi>log</mi><mfrac><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>γ</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{split}D_{\text{KL}}(P\parallel H^{\gamma}):=\int_{y\in{\mathbb{R}^{M}}}\log{\frac{P(dy)}{H^{\gamma}(dy)}}\,P(dy).\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.p4.2" class="ltx_p">Its parameter-derivative is</p>
<table id="S5.Ex53" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex53.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{split}\delta D_{\text{KL}}(P\parallel H^{\gamma})=-\int_{y\in{\mathbb{R}^{M}}}\frac{\delta H^{\gamma}(dy)}{H^{\gamma}(dy)}\,P(dy)=-\int_{y\in{\mathbb{R}^{M}}}\frac{\delta h^{\gamma}}{h^{\gamma}}(y)\,P(dy)\\
=-\int_{y\in{\mathbb{R}^{M}}}\delta\log h^{\gamma}(y)\,P(dy)=-\frac{1}{N_{data}}\sum_{k=1}^{N_{data}}\delta\log h^{\gamma}(y_{k}).\end{split}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mi>D</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mtext>KL</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><mi>H</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>γ</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.055em">−</mo><mo>∫</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>M</mi></msup></mrow></msub><mfrac><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>γ</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>γ</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.055em">−</mo><mo>∫</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>M</mi></msup></mrow></msub><mfrac><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>h</mi><mi>γ</mi></msup></mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><mi>γ</mi></msup></mfrac><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo rspace="0.340em" stretchy="false">)</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.055em">−</mo><mo>∫</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>M</mi></msup></mrow></msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>log</mi><mi>h</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>γ</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo rspace="0.340em" stretchy="false">)</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msub><mi>N</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow></msub></mfrac><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow></msub></msup><mi>δ</mi><mi>log</mi><mi>h</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>γ</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{split}\delta D_{\text{KL}}(P\parallel H^{\gamma})=-\int_{y\in{\mathbb{R}^{M}}}\frac{\delta H^{\gamma}(dy)}{H^{\gamma}(dy)}\,P(dy)=-\int_{y\in{\mathbb{R}^{M}}}\frac{\delta h^{\gamma}}{h^{\gamma}}(y)\,P(dy)\\
=-\int_{y\in{\mathbb{R}^{M}}}\delta\log h^{\gamma}(y)\,P(dy)=-\frac{1}{N_{data}}\sum_{k=1}^{N_{data}}\delta\log h^{\gamma}(y_{k}).\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.p4.3" class="ltx_p">With the new formula in <a href="#S1.SS1" title="1.1. Main results ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Divergence-Kernel method for linear responses and diffusion models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.1</span></a>, we have an expression of <math id="S5.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta\log h^{\gamma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msup><mi>h</mi><mi>γ</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta\log h^{\gamma}</annotation></semantics></math> at <math id="S5.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{T,l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{T,l}</annotation></semantics></math>’s, which enables the gradient optimization on this KL divergence.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Then we need to decide how to compute <math id="S5.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta\log h^{\gamma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msup><mi>h</mi><mi>γ</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta\log h^{\gamma}</annotation></semantics></math> at the prescribed locations <math id="S5.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>’s.
It is difficult to obtain paths ending exactly at <math id="S5.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>, so we need an approximation.
We approximate <math id="S5.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> by its <math id="S5.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{neighbor}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{neighbor}</annotation></semantics></math>-nearest neighbors in <math id="S5.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{x_{T,l}\}_{l=1}^{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>L</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{x_{T,l}\}_{l=1}^{L}</annotation></semantics></math>, and therefore <math id="S5.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta\log h^{\gamma}(y_{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msup><mi>h</mi><mi>γ</mi></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta\log h^{\gamma}(y_{k})</annotation></semantics></math> is approximated by the average of the divergence-kernel expression in <a href="#S1.SS1" title="1.1. Main results ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Divergence-Kernel method for linear responses and diffusion models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.1</span></a> over those <math id="S5.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{neighbor}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{neighbor}</annotation></semantics></math> paths.
Hence,</p>
<table id="S5.Ex54" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex54.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{split}\delta D_{\text{KL}}(P\parallel H^{\gamma})\approx\hat{\delta}D_{\text{KL}}(P\parallel H^{\gamma}):=-\frac{1}{N_{data}}\sum_{k=1}^{N_{data}}\frac{1}{N_{neighbor}}\sum_{x_{T,l}\in N(y_{k})}\mathcal{T}(x_{T,l}),\end{split}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable displaystyle="true"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mi>D</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mtext>KL</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><mi>H</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>γ</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>≈</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>δ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>D</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mtext>KL</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><mi>H</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>γ</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo rspace="0em">:=</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msub><mi>N</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow></msub></mfrac><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow></msub></msup><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msub><mi>N</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub></mfrac><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{split}\delta D_{\text{KL}}(P\parallel H^{\gamma})\approx\hat{\delta}D_{\text{KL}}(P\parallel H^{\gamma}):=-\frac{1}{N_{data}}\sum_{k=1}^{N_{data}}\frac{1}{N_{neighbor}}\sum_{x_{T,l}\in N(y_{k})}\mathcal{T}(x_{T,l}),\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.p5.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S5.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N(y_{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N(y_{k})</annotation></semantics></math> is the neighbors of <math id="S5.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S5.p5.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}(x_{T,l})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}(x_{T,l})</annotation></semantics></math> is the expression in <a href="#S1.SS1" title="1.1. Main results ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Divergence-Kernel method for linear responses and diffusion models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.1</span></a> on the path ending at <math id="S5.p5.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{T,l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{T,l}</annotation></semantics></math>.
After approximately computing the parameter-derivative, we update the parameter by gradient descent,</p>
<table id="S5.Ex55" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex55.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{split}\gamma_{n+1}=\gamma_{n}-\eta\hat{\delta}D_{\text{KL}}(P\parallel H^{\gamma}).\end{split}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable displaystyle="true"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mi>γ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>γ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>η</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>δ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>D</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mtext>KL</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><mi>H</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>γ</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{split}\gamma_{n+1}=\gamma_{n}-\eta\hat{\delta}D_{\text{KL}}(P\parallel H^{\gamma}).\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.p5.3" class="ltx_p">For simplicity, we keep <math id="S5.p5.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>η</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta</annotation></semantics></math> constant, but we can also change it after each update of <math id="S5.p5.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.1. </span>A 1-d problem with 2 parameters</h3>

<div id="S5.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">First, we try a 1-dimensional problem.
The SDE is</p>
<table id="S5.Ex56" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex56.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{split}dx^{i}=\left(\gamma^{0}-x\right)dt+\left(0.5+\exp\left(-(x-\gamma^{1})^{2}\right)\right)dB,\quad\textnormal{where}\quad x_{0}\sim{\mathcal{N}}(0,1).\end{split}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable displaystyle="true"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>γ</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mn>0.5</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>γ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mtext>where</mtext><mspace style="width:2.000em;" width="2.000em"></mspace><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{split}dx^{i}=\left(\gamma^{0}-x\right)dt+\left(0.5+\exp\left(-(x-\gamma^{1})^{2}\right)\right)dB,\quad\textnormal{where}\quad x_{0}\sim{\mathcal{N}}(0,1).\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">The true parameter used to generate the data is <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{true}=[0,0]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{true}=[0,0]</annotation></semantics></math>.
The initial parameter in the optimization is <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{0}=[5,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{0}=[5,1]</annotation></semantics></math>.
The number of data points is <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{data}=200" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>200</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{data}=200</annotation></semantics></math>.
The number of samples generated for each update of <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L=200" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>200</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L=200</annotation></semantics></math>.
The stepsize (or learning rate) is <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta=1</annotation></semantics></math>.
The number of neighbors used to approximate each <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{neighbor}=5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{neighbor}=5</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We start from <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>γ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> and use gradient descent to minimize the KL-divergence.
The histograms of <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>’s and <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{T,l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{T,l}</annotation></semantics></math>’s for <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>γ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{10}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>γ</mi><mn>10</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{10}</annotation></semantics></math>, the parameter after 10 updates, are shown in <a href="#S5.F3" title="In 5.1. A 1-d problem with 2 parameters ‣ 5. A simple forward-only diffusion model ‣ Divergence-Kernel method for linear responses and diffusion models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>.
As we can see, the two distributions get much closer during the optimization.
More specifically, the error <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma-\gamma_{true}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma-\gamma_{true}</annotation></semantics></math> is reduced from <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[5,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[5,1]</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[-0.05,0.09]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>0.05</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>0.09</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[-0.05,0.09]</annotation></semantics></math>.
The computational time for all 10 updates on a 4-core CPU is about 2 minutes, but note that our implementation is far from optimal.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.F3" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2"><img src="2509.03992v1/figs/gm_1d_hist_0.png" id="S5.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_figure_panel ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:187/191;" width="187" height="191" alt="Refer to caption"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2"><img src="2509.03992v1/figs/gm_1d_hist_10.png" id="S5.F3.g2" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_figure_panel ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:187/191;" width="187" height="191" alt="Refer to caption"></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S5.F3.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 3</span>. </span><span id="S5.F3.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">
Histograms of <math id="S5.F3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>’s and <math id="S5.F3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{T,l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{T,l}</annotation></semantics></math>’s.
Left: plot at <math id="S5.F3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>γ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Right: at <math id="S5.F3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{10}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>γ</mi><mn>10</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{10}</annotation></semantics></math>.
</span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.2. </span>A 5-d problem with 6 parameters</h3>

<div id="S5.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We try a 5-dimensional Lorenz 96 system.
The SDE is</p>
<table id="S5.EGx4" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_eqnarray ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S5.Ex57"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.Ex57.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle dx^{i}=\left(\left(x^{i+1}-x^{i-2}\right)x^{i-1}-x^{i}+\gamma^{i}-0.01(x^{i})^{2}\right)dt+\left(0.5+\exp\left((x-\gamma^{M}\mathbbm{1})^{2}\right)\right)dB^{i}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>γ</mi><mi>i</mi></msup></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>0.01</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mn>0.5</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><msup><mi>γ</mi><mi>M</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>𝟙</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>B</mi><mi>i</mi></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle dx^{i}=\left(\left(x^{i+1}-x^{i-2}\right)x^{i-1}-x^{i}+\gamma^{i}-0.01(x^{i})^{2}\right)dt+\left(0.5+\exp\left((x-\gamma^{M}\mathbbm{1})^{2}\right)\right)dB^{i},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S5.Ex58"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.Ex58.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\textnormal{where}\quad i=0,\ldots,M-1;\quad M=5;\quad\mathbbm{1}=[1,1,1,1,1];\quad x_{0}\sim{\mathcal{N}}(0,I)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mtext>where</mtext><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">;</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">;</mo><mrow><mn>𝟙</mn><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">;</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\textnormal{where}\quad i=0,\ldots,M-1;\quad M=5;\quad\mathbbm{1}=[1,1,1,1,1];\quad x_{0}\sim{\mathcal{N}}(0,I).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">The true parameter used to generate the data is <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{true}=[5,6,7,8,9,2]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>6</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>7</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>8</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>9</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{true}=[5,6,7,8,9,2]</annotation></semantics></math>.
The initial parameter in the optimization is <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{0}=[0,0,0,0,0,0]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{0}=[0,0,0,0,0,0]</annotation></semantics></math>.
The number of data points is <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{data}=200" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>200</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{data}=200</annotation></semantics></math>.
The number of samples generated for each update of <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L=200" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>200</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L=200</annotation></semantics></math>.
The stepsize (or learning rate) is <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta=1</annotation></semantics></math>.
The number of neighbors used to approximate each <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{neighbor}=5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{neighbor}=5</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We start from <math id="S5.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>γ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> and use gradient descent to minimize the KL-divergence.
The history of the <math id="S5.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>l</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>-distance <math id="S5.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\gamma_{n}-\gamma_{true}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\gamma_{n}-\gamma_{true}|</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S5.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=0</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S5.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="50" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>50</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">50</annotation></semantics></math> is shown in <a href="#S5.F4" title="In 5.2. A 5-d problem with 6 parameters ‣ 5. A simple forward-only diffusion model ‣ Divergence-Kernel method for linear responses and diffusion models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>.
As we can see, <math id="S5.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> gets closer to <math id="S5.SS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{true}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>γ</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{true}</annotation></semantics></math> during optimization, with <math id="S5.SS2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{48}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>γ</mi><mn>48</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{48}</annotation></semantics></math> achieving the minimum distance.
More specifically,</p>
<table id="S5.Ex59" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex59.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{split}\gamma_{0}-\gamma_{true}=[-5,-6,-7,-8,-9,-2];\\
\gamma_{48}-\gamma_{true}=[-0.33,0.40,-0.26,-0.20,-0.24,-0.36].\end{split}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>7</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>9</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>;</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mn>48</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>0.33</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>0.40</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>0.26</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>0.20</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>0.24</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>0.36</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{split}\gamma_{0}-\gamma_{true}=[-5,-6,-7,-8,-9,-2];\\
\gamma_{48}-\gamma_{true}=[-0.33,0.40,-0.26,-0.20,-0.24,-0.36].\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">The computational time for all 50 updates on a 4-core CPU is about 2 minutes, but note that our implementation is far from optimal.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.F4" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.03992v1/figs/gm_lorenz.png" id="S5.F4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:235/191;" width="235" height="191" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S5.F4.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 4</span>. </span><span id="S5.F4.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">
History of <math id="S5.F4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\gamma_{n}-\gamma_{true}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\gamma_{n}-\gamma_{true}|</annotation></semantics></math> for the 5-d diffusion model.
</span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="Sx1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section">Declarations</h2>

<section id="Sx1.SSx1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">Data availability statement</h3>

<div id="Sx1.SSx1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.SSx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The code used in this paper is posted at <a href="https://github.com/niangxiu/DKlinR" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_url ltx_font_typewriter">https://github.com/niangxiu/DKlinR</a>.
There are no other associated data.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Sx1.SSx2" class="ltx_subsection">
